## Contents
## 1. Introduction
In the face of severe energy and environmental issues today, people’s thirst for innovative alternative energy systems is increasingly strong. To save energy and protect the ecological environment, fuel cells (FCs) that can efficiently transform chemical energy directly into electric energy have attracted considerable attention [1,2]. In this context, proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs) using a proton exchange membrane (PEM) as a proton-transfering medium have the advantages of fast start-up speed, modular installation, easy operation, and pollution-free. It is worth noting that as the core component of PEMFCs, the proton conductor dominates their performance and service life. In addition, proton conductor shows great potential applications in other fields such as chemosensors [3–8] proton transistors and sieving [9,10] and so on [11–14]. Anyway, the most important use of proton conductors is still in the field of PEMFCs now.
To date, perfluorosulfonic acid proton exchange membranes, such as Nafion and Nafion-like membranes are the most widely used commercial PEMs in PEMFCs due to their advantages of excellent chemical and thermal stability, high proton conductivity $\mathbf{\Pi}(\upsigma\mathbf{\Lambda})$ and high mechanical strength [15,16]. The large amount of sulfonic acid group carried by these PEMs ensures high proton conductive efficiency with the assistance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units [17]. Nevertheless, in the process of developing and using the PEMs mentioned above, people have realized that such PEMs have such disadvantages as high cost, rigorous use conditions (such as the need for high humidity and limited working temperature range: $60-80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , etc., which limits their further application. A more fatal flaw is that the structures of these polymers are fuzzy or semi-crystalline, which is not conducive to researchers’ in-depth understanding of the proton conductive mechanism, thus leading to limited performance improvement. Therefore, the actual needs and future development drive us to broaden our research field and look for more proton conductive materials, so that they can be used as alternative materials and overcome the shortcomings of existing PEMs, such as high cost and amorphous structures.
As concluded by H. Kitagawa and co-workers [18] the proton transfer in solids across H-bonded networks was scheduled in the early 1950s [14]. Thus, the application of solid-state crystalline materials such as metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [19–35] covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [36–38] and hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks (HOFs) [39–44] with ample and intricate Hbonded networks in the field of proton conduction has attracted the strong attention of researchers all over the world. Of course, the high crystallinity, porosity, and chemical modifiability of these solid materials provide them with unlimited potential in the field of proton conductivity.
In this review, we focus our attention on a class of important crystalline MOFs materials. During the past 20 years, MOFs assembled from metal-ions/clusters and bridging ligands have aroused wide concern in many fields, like gas sorption/separation [45–47] sensor [48–52] photoluminescence [53–55] catalysts [30,56] and so on [57,58]. The above studies have shown that the variable structure of MOFs directly leads to their multiple applications, that is, the structure determines the properties. It is well known that the structures of organic ligands, the rigidity and flexibility of the skeleton, and the functional groups carried by organic ligands play a decisive role in the structures of subsequent MOFs. Therefore, chemists can regulate the fine structure of MOFs utilizing molecular design and molecular tailoring. At the same time, the shape and size of the pores in the frameworks as well as the chemical composition can be carefully regulated. All of these provide a solid material basis for the use of these MOFs as proton conducting materials. Besides, the high crystallinity of such materials, especially with the development of single-crystal growing technology, makes it feasible to further explore the fine proton-conducting mechanism occurring within the frameworks. Finally, the abundant pore channels in the MOFs are very helpful for the introduction of the various conducting medium, eg. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ [29,33,35,59–64] nonvolatile acids [65,66] and protonic organic molecules [67–69] to enhance the proton conductivity of the MOFs.
Stimulated by the mentioned structural advantages of MOFs, people began to study the proton conductivity of such MOFs as early as 1979 [70]. The first example of the proton conductive MOFs is a two-dimensional $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ MOF [70] but its conducting mechanism was indistinct due to the low crystallinity of this MOF. It was not until 2009 that Kitagawa and Shimizu teams had a relatively clear comprehension of the proton-conducting mechanisms through the in-depth study of the crystalline MOFs [71–74]. Summarizing the implementation strategy, proton-conductive MOFs can be segmented into four categories. Type I includes the charged frameworks, in which the void space exists the form of gegenion, such as $\mathrm{H}_{3}0^{+}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+},$ , and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ , which is generated synchronically during MOF synthesis [5,75–79]. Types II and III are composed of the functional structural elements, such as the acidic group in the organic ligand (carboxylate, sulfonate and phosphate groups, etc.) or coordination interpolation of functional molecule in metal-center (water, ethanol and $N\mathrm{.}$ -heterocyclic units, etc.), which can be achieved by using preliminary design [80,81] or post-synthetic modification [82,83]. Type IV contains guest units (acidic units, protonic organic units, polyoxometalate, and so on), which shows a common way of utilizing MOFs porousness and producing protons [84–88].
With the investigation of proton-conductive MOFs, the conduction mechanism is revealed in detail. The usual proton-conduction mechanism is vastly explored using water as the proton conduction media. Given the diffusion motion of protons, two major proton-conduction mechanisms are recognized: the Grutthuss mechanism of proton hopping diffusion between conductive medium together with pivoted redirection motion, and the vehicle mechanism of the diffusion of protonic categories from high to low concentration [11,18,21,89]. Moreover, the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ covering the hydrogen-bond formation and dissociation energy provides intuitional signals for inference of the conduction-mechanism: Grutthuss $(E_{\mathrm{a}}<0.4~\mathrm{eV})$ and the vehicle mechanism $(E_{\mathrm{a}}>0.4~\mathrm{eV})$ , which is figured out by fitting the temperature-dependent proton conductivity $\scriptstyle(\sigma\ )$ into the Arrhenius’ equation.
As mentioned above, sulfonic acid groups play a pivotal role in the hydrophilicity and high proton conductivity of Nafion films. Therefore, in the field of MOFs, it is expected to continue to maintain the functional advantages of sulfonic groups. Compared with carboxylic acid and phosphoric acid, sulfonic acid has the smallest pKa value and is the most acidic, making it a good proton source. The three oxygen atoms of sulfonic acid can participate in the coordination of metal ions in different directions, thus forming different structural (1D, 2D, or 3D) and stable MOFs. Note that as a weak Brønsted base, organosulfonate indicates weak coordination ability toward many metal ions, and therefore difficult to be introduced into MOFs [90]. However, under the synergistic action of other functional groups, such as carboxyl and phosphate groups, it is relatively easy to obtain MOFs bearing free sulfonic acid groups, which is conducive to proton transport. The reason is that the three oxygen atoms of the sulfonic acid group are good hydrogen bond receptors and have a strong ability to form H-bonds. They can accept three hydrogen bond donors at the same time and form a divergent H-bonded structure to facilitate proton transmission. Thus, sulfonate-functionalized MOFs as a significant type of proton-conductive MOFs materials have received much attention. The robust metal sulfonates can afford an inherent proton source, either to partially protonize the sulfo-groups at low pH or by introducing acidic functional-groups, making them a prospective application of proton-conduction material.
Although there have been many recent reviews on the research progress of proton conductive MOFs [18–21,25,28–30,34,66,90–9 1] such as on proton conductive carboxylate-based MOFs [22] and phosphate-based MOFs [65] no review on proton conductive sulfonate-based MOFs has been published. Herein, we provide the review to fill this vacancy and enhance understanding of the proton conduction in sulfonate MOFs, promoting people to carry out more research and design more high-performance proton conduction materials.
## 2. Proton conductive sulfonate MOFs
## 2.1. Pristine MOFs with sulfonic acid groups
Recently, the sulfonate ligands have received extensive attention for construction of multiple structures. The strongly polar –
$S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups are usually introduced into the frameworks of MOFs to functionalize the pore surfaces, thereby improving the intrinsic proton conductivity. The acidic $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups in sulfonate ligands have relatively strong coordination ability, which enriches the number of possible geometric configurations between O-donors and metal-ions, thus forming a polynuclear structure. Besides, the acidic $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups in pores can construct ample H-bonded networks to enhance proton conduction.
## 2.1.1. MOFs constructed by coordinated sulfonic acid groups
In general, the $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ group in sulfonate MOFs tends to present as monoanion to coordinate with metal centers. In particular, since the sulfonic group has three O atoms, the sulfonate unit can connect the metals into a multi-dimensional framework, while the O atoms can further act as hydrogen-bond receptors to anchor proton carriers or to transfer protons.
The bifunctional ligands bearing both $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and –COOH groups have received increasing interest recently because of their strong coordination ability and versatile coordination modes. In 2019, by using a bifunctional ligand, 4-sulfobenzoic acid $(4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{s}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{a})$ and an indispensable auxiliary ligand bipyridine-glycoluril (bpg) bearing four $\mathsf{H}$ -bonding donors (NH) and two H-bonding accepters $({\mathsf{C}}=0)$ ), Kumbhar et al. successfully fabricated a proton conductive 3D MOF, $\{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathsf{s b a})_{2}(\mathsf{b p g})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}].5\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-1) [92]. As denoted in Fig. 1, each doubly deprotonated $\mathsf{s b a}^{2-}$ ligand bridges the neighboring ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ atoms to build up a chain. At the same time, bpg ligand chelates the central ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ion. Furthermore, these chains are stacked into a 3D solid-state structure by intermolecular H-bonds with 1D porous channels. Note that in this framework, there are a large number of coordination and crystallization $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units, uncoordinated oxygen atoms from carboxyl and sulfonic acid groups, and NH and $C=0$ groups of bpg ligands, which are very conducive to forming a dense, complicated and ordered H-bonded network for efficient transmission of protons.
For the water-assisted proton conduction studies, the primary concern is the water stability of the MOFs. Therefore, the assynthesized sample of S-1 was placed under $95\%$ RH for 2 days and afterwards refluxed in $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for 1 day. Moreover, the sample was dipped in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for 30 days at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ . Finally, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) test of the sample acquired after the above treatment demonstrates that their patterns are close to those of the samples before treatment and the simulated pattern obtained through single-crystal data, which confirms their super high water stability. Consequently, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) determinations of the pellet sample for this MOF revealed that its $\upsigma$ value increases with temperature and humidity. Under $80~^{\circ}C$ and $95\%$ relative humidity (RH), the optimal $\upsigma$ of $9.4\times10^{-3}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ can be got, which is a remarkable $\upsigma$ about proton conducting MOFs to now. At the same time, after up to $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ of testing, the proton conductivity of the MOF remained basically constant. To reveal why the compound exhibits a high $\upsigma$ , the authors further tested its water vapor adsorption, calculated the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value by experimental data, and simulated the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value using theoretical calculations. This MOF denotes a prominent $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption amount of $379.07~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ as $P/P_{0}$ being 0.95 at room temperature, which indicates that there are a great amount of hydrophilic components, and in fact the structural analysis mentioned above also confirms this judgment. Further electrochemical experiment of this MOF manifested that the MOF had a lower $\upsigma$ value under ${\sf D}_{2}0$ vapor than that under $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor. These experiments fully demonstrated that the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units acts as a critical role in proton conducting process. The authors concluded that there is mainly a Grotthuss mechanism with a slight vehicular mechanism in accordance with the experimental $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value $(0.64~\mathrm{eV})$ . In general, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $\mathsf{\Omega}<0.4\mathrm{\eV}$ can be inferred as Grotthuss mechanism, which contradicts their conclusions, which illustrates the complexity of proton conduction mechanism research, and obviously needs more experimental data for further judgment. Nevertheless, the high $\upsigma$ of this highly waterstable MOF using the bifunctional ligand containing both sulfonic acid and carboxylic acid groups provides ample confidence for further synthesis of such useful materials.
As early as 2015, Zhang group used a similar multifunctional ligand, 5-sulfoisophthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}5\mathsf{-}\mathsf{s i p})$ bearing two –COOH and one $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups to prepare a 3D conductive copper(II) MOF, $\mathrm{Cu}_{4}(5{\-}{\cdot}{\mathrm{sip}})_{2}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}({\mathrm{DMF}})_{2}$ (S-2) $(\mathrm{DMF}\ =\ N,N.$ -dimethylformamide) [93]. The distinctive structural feature of this MOF is that four copper ions form a tetranuclear cluster across the connection of four carboxyl groups and two sulfonic acid groups from six organic ligands, and these clusters form a 3D non-interpenetrating structure by the further connection of organic ligands with 1D irregular channels with a diameter of about $7.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ In addition, although all O atoms of the organic ligands coordinated to the copper ion, some of them can establish abound H-bonded networks (C-H O) with the C atoms of DMF units in the channels, although these H-bonds are somewhat weak. The $\upsigma$ value of this MOF soared with the increase of temperature and RH. A maximum $\upsigma$ value $(7.4\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm})$ can be attained at $95^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $95\%$ RH. The higher $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value $(1.32\mathrm{eV})$ suggests that the H-bonds in the channels may not form efficient proton transportation pathways. So, the proton transport of this MOF is mainly governed by the vehicle mechanism.
By analyzing the structures, it is easy to understand why proton conductivity of S-2 is weaker than that of S-1. The oxygen atoms of the organic ligand of S-2 all participate in coordination, so merely weak H-bonded system can be established, but there are a mass of uncoordinated oxygen atoms in S-1, which can constitute a strong and dense H-bonded system more conducive to the proton transfer.
In 2017 and 2019, by adopting ${\mathsf{N a}}_{3}5$ -sip and several $N\cdot$ containing ligands, Ghoshal group continuously prepared a series of MOFs under mild condtions, 1D $\{[\mathsf{Z n}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpeH})(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-3) $(4,4^{\prime}{\-}\mathsf{b p e}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bispyridylethane), 1D $\{[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{p y z})(\mathsf{H}5-$ sip) $)(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]\cdot(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-4) (pyz $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ pyrazine), 1D $\{|\mathsf{C u}(4,4^{\prime}-$ bpee) $)_{0.5}(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpeeH}_{2})_{0.5}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-5) ( $^{\cdot}4,4^{\prime}$ -bpee $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bispyridylethylene), 1D $\{[\mathrm{Cu}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})(\mathrm{H}5{\cdot}\mathrm{sip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)]{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-6) $(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy}\ =\ 4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine), 2D $\{[\mathsf{C u}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}5\mathrm{-}\mathsf{s i p})_{2}]$  $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{6}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-7) [94] and 2D $\{[\mathsf{C d}-(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpe})_{0.5}(5\mathrm{-}\mathsf{s i p})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(4,4^{\prime}{\mathrm{-bpeH}}_{2})_{0.5}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-8), 2D $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{1.5}(\mathsf{b t e})(5-\mathsf{s i p})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-9) (bte $\mathit{\Theta}=1{,}2$ -bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)ethane) and 2D $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{1.5}(\mathsf{b t p})(5\mathrm{-}\mathsf{s i p})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}})_{3}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-10) (btp = 1,3-bis(1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)propane) [95]. It should be pointed out that for the convenience of the discussion of proton conductivity, this series of structurally similar compounds is compared in this chapter, although several MOFs have free sulfonate groups (S-3, S-4 and S-6). To compare the proton conductivity of S-3-S-10, these compounds with similar components are discussed in this chapter, although S-3 and S-4 have free sulfonate groups. As indicated in Fig. 2, In S-3, two carboxylate units of one $5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip}^{3\mathrm{-}}$ ligand joined neighboring ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ cations to form an infinite chain and the monoprotonated $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpeH ligands hang on this chain by coordination with the central metal atoms. In S-4, pyz ligands bridged neighboring ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ cations to make up a chain and $5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hsip}^{2\mathrm{-}}$ ligand used one carboxylate unit to chelate the ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ cation. Unlike S-3, S-4 contains free sulfonate groups as well as free –COOH groups. S-5 and S-6 are ladder-like chain structures. In S-5, one $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ unit and one $-S0_{3}^{-}$ unit of $5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip}^{3\mathrm{-}}$ ligands and $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -containing ligands are involved in linking with the $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ ions, so each ${5\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{sip}}^{3\mathrm{-}}$ ligand bears one free carboxylate unit. In S-6, one COOH unit and one $-S0_{3}^{-}$ unit of each ${\mathsf{H}}5{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{s i p}}^{2-}$ ligand did not take part in the coordination with the $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ ions. In S-7, the $-S0_{3}^{-}$ units connected the adjacent ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ cations to build up a chain, and then the chain was linked by $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy to establish a grid-like structure, in which the $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ units are free. Unlike S-7, all $-\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ and $-S0_{3}^{-}$ units in 2D MOFs, S-8-S-10 bridge the metal ions. Note that there are infinite 1D channels filled with water molecules in the solid-state packing structures of these MOFs. In addition, the authors did not mention the water stability of the above MOFs.
Because there are vast coordination and crystallization $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units, and carboxylate and sulfonate groups in these frameworks, a complex H-bonded network can be established, which is very convenient for proton transport. Alternating current (AC) impedance investigations by pelletized sample of these MOFs denoted that their $\upsigma$ values are temperature- and humidity-dependent, and are from $10^{-5}$ to $10^{-8}\mathrm{\:S/cm}$ under $65~^{\circ}C$ and $95\%$ RH. Apparently, these values are ordinary. Nevertheless, from the analysis of the relationship between structure and performance, people can get inspiration for further research. For example, at $65~^{\circ}C$ and $95\%$ RH, because S-4 has one protonated –COOH unit, its $\upsigma$ value $(3.5~\times~10^{-5}~\mathrm{S/cm})$ is ten times higher than that of S-3 $(2.5\times10^{-6}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ under the same conditions. Similarly, S-6 contains one COOH unit and one $-S0_{3}^{-}$ unit, so its $\upsigma$ $(5.8\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S}/$ cm) is much higher than that $(9.9\times10^{-8}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ of S-5, which only includes one $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ unit. S-7 also has two carboxylate units, so its $\upsigma$ value is close to that of S-6. S-8 bears protonated free $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -H2bpe ligands benefiting the proton conduction, so its $\upsigma$ value $(3.7\times10^{-5}$ $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m})$ is greater than that of S-9 $(4.6~\times~10^{-7}~\mathrm{S/cm})$ and S-10 $(3.9\times10^{-6}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ . The $\upsigma$ value of S-10 is slightly higher than that of S-9, which may be due to the flexibility of the btp ligand in S-10 over that in S-9. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of these MOFs are close to $0.4~\mathrm{eV}$ · except for S-3 $(0.54~\mathrm{eV})$ , so their proton conduction mechanism basically obeys the Grotthuss mechanism. As for S-3, its slightly higher $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value may be because of the enhanced contribution of the vehicle proton transport.
Wang et al. solvothermally synthesized a 2D planar Eu-MOF, $[\{(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O})[\mathrm{Eu(SBDB)(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\}_{\mathrm{n}}]$ (S-11) by adopting a multi-functional ligand, 1,5-disulfo-benzene-2,4-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}S B D B)}$ in 2018 [96]. The Eu(III) atoms are linked by O atoms from the carboxylate and sulfonic groups to construct a layer, and thereafter joined by H-bonds arising from $\mathrm{H}_{3}0^{+}$ ions and O atoms to from a 3D supramolecular framework bearing 1D channels filled with $S=0$ units. This MOF solely loses water units in the range $25-$ $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ indicating high thermal stability. At the same time, S-11 holds its structural rigidity after soaking the MOF in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ or placing under $98\%$ RH for $3{\mathrm{~d~}}$ . Its $\upsigma$ values are relevant with RH and temperature meaning that as the humidity and temperature increase, the proton conductivity also increases. At $65~^{\circ}C$ and $98\%$ RH, the highest $\upsigma$ value of S-11 attains $1.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S/cm}$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is 0.48 eV illustrating that a mixing Grotthuss and Vehicle proton conducting mechanisms can be discovered. Apparently, the $S_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{BDB}^{4-}$ anions can comprise H-bonds across $-\mathsf{S O}_{3}^{-...}\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{O}^{+}\cdot\cdot\cdot(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{\mathrm{n}}$ units for protons hopping. In the case of high humidity, such proton hopping process is accompanied by the diffusion process of some protons with water molecules.
In 2009, Shimizu and co-workers reported an interesting 3D honeycomb MOF, $\mathsf{N a}_{3}(2,4,6$ -trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate), namely $\beta$ -PCMOF2 [97] by employing 2,4,6-trihydroxy-1,3,5-ben zenetrisulfonate (THB). Notably, the framework of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 contains regular 1D pores (diameter: $5.65(2){-}5.91(2)\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ lined exclusively with O atoms of sulfonate units. The authors investigated its proton conducting properties under anhydrous $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ gas. The $\upsigma$ decreased with the increase of temperature $(30\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ $\mathrm{~\textit~{~\textcent~}~}=3.7\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S/cm}$ ; above $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ : $\upsigma\ <10^{-8}\ S/\mathrm{cm})$ , which was caused by the decrease of hydration degree of the MOF. To meet the requirements of high temperature conditions in PEMs and direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs), the authors hoped to replace the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units in the MOF with $N$ -heterocyclic units, for instance, 1H-1,2,4-triazole $(\mathsf{T}\boldsymbol{z})$ , to study their high temperature conductivity. Therefore, they doped ${\sf T}z$ in situ in the $\mathfrak{a}$ -phase and converted it into $\upbeta$ -phase by heating. PXRD test denoted that the structure of the compound did not change before and after doping. The dopant is named $\beta{\mathbf{-PCMOF}}2(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{z})_{\mathbf{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{x}}$ presenting the loading amount of $\mathrm{T}z$ ). The $\upsigma$ value of $\beta{\mathbf{-PCMOF}}2(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{z})_{\mathbf{x}}$ is greater than that of the unloaded, hydrated counterpart. The $\upsigma$ values of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 $(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{Z})_{0.3}$ , $\beta$ -PCMOF2(Tz)0.45, $\mathbf{\beta}\mathbf{-PCMOF2(TZ)_{0.60}}$ are $2\times\begin{array}{l l}{10^{-4}}\end{array}$ $5\times10^{-4}$ and $4\times10^{-4}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , respectively. These values are much higher than either pure ${\sf T}z$ or pure $\beta$ -PCMOF2. PXRD patterns confirmed the retention of the loaded MOFs after the impedance test. As ${\sf T}z$ loading amount augments, the slope of the Arrhenius plot is changed $[\mathbf{x}=0.3$ , $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.51$ eV $(23-150~^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ ; x $=0.45$ , $E_{\mathrm{a}}=1.8\textrm{e}$ V $(50-90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ or 0.34 eV $(90-150~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ; $\textbf{\textit{X}}=0.6$ $E_{\mathrm{a}}~=~1.87$ eV $(23-80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ or 0.56 eV $(80-150~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ]. Under the condition of high temperature and high doping amount, the decrease of the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the compound may be due to the phase transfer and with the increase of loadings; it is more conducive to the formation of efficient H-bonded proton transport channel within the framework.
Three years later, the same group enhanced the proton conductivity of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 via isomorphous ligand replacement approach [98]. They believe that since both the ligands THB and 1,3,5- benzenetriphosphonic acid $\mathrm{(H_{6}B T P)}$ have a single aromatic core and three hydrophilic substituents $\mathrm{\hbar}.-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $\mathrm{\Gamma}-\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\dot{\Gamma}}$ ) showing $C_{3}$ symmetry, and can be used for isomorphous replacement to boost the $\upsigma$ values of the resulting substituents. So the MOF with mixed ligands of THB and $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T P}$ was obtained and named PCMOF2- $^{1/2}$ . Thereafter, humidity-dependent proton conductivity of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 and PCMOF2-1/2 was explored. The $\upsigma$ value of the two compounds enhances with upraising RH and temperature. Under $85~^{\circ}C$ and $90\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ value of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 is $1\cdot{\overset{-}{3}}\times10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ , while the value of PCMOF2- $^{1/2}$ with one-third of THB was replaced by $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTP}$ can attain $2.1\times10^{-2}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under the identical conditions. This superior conductivity can be attributed to the substitution ligands having more acidic protons. The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of $\beta$ -PCMOF2 and PCMOF2- $^{1/2}$ are 0.28 and $0.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, demonstrating a Grotthuss mechanism for proton transfer.
Consequently, the same group loaded heterocyclic proton carriers into PCMOF2- $^{1/2}$ or $\beta$ -PCMOF2 to produce dopants, PCMOF2- $\mathbf{1}/2(\mathbf{Pz})$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{'Pz}}&{{}=}&{1H\mathrm{.}}\end{array}$ -pyrazole), PCMOF2- ${\bf1/2(T z)}$ and PCMOF2 $(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{Z})$ (Fig. 3), and discovered that the $\upsigma$ values of the loading MOFs were further improved by almost two orders of magnitude [99]. Note that the structures of doped MOFs are similar to PCMOF2- $^{1/2}$ and $\beta$ -PCMOF2. The $\upsigma$ value of the generated MOFs was higher than $10^{-1}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ under $85\ ^{\circ}C$ and $90\%$ RH [PCMOF2- $\mathbf{1}/2(\mathbf{Pz})$ : $1.1~\times~10^{-1}~\mathsf{S/c m}$ ; PCMOF2- $1/2$ $\mathbf{\Gamma}(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{z})$ : $1.17\times10^{-1}\colon$ S/cm; PCMOF2 $(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z})$ : $4.6\times10^{-2}\ S/\mathrm{cm})$ . Their $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are 0.16, 0.22 and $0.10\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, showing the characteristic water-assisted proton hopping mechanism. By comparing the values of these activation energies and the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ values of the doped heterocyclic molecules, the authors suggest that low activation energies do not necessarily lead to high proton conductivity. Proton conductivity can be influenced by a variety of factors, such as the position of the proton donor and acceptor within the framework, host–guest interactions, the size of the heterocycles, and $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ value. Moreover, the anhydrous $\upsigma$ values of PCMOF2- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{1}/2(\mathbf{Pz})$ and PCMOF2 $(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z})$ are $7.2\times10^{-7}$ , and $1.3\times10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ , respectively under $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air. In general, the loaded $\mathsf{P}z$ and Tz units are helpful to generate an effective proton transfer pathway under hydrous or anhydrous conditions, which is a highly efficient way to improve the proton conductivity of the resulting MOFs.
Another similar 3D THB-based MOF, $(\mathsf{C s}_{3}(\mathrm{THB})(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)_{3.4}$ (S-12) with 1-D hydration pores occupied by crystallization $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units $(6.52\times7.71\mathrm{~\AA})$ was synthesized by Shimizu et. al in 2015 [100]. Because this MOF is unstable at high temperature $(>80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and high humidity $(>90\%$ RH), the authors only reported the $\upsigma$ value under a moderate humidity ( $50\%$ RH) and $25\mathrm{-}70~^{\circ}C$ . The best proton conductivity of S-12 reached $1.1\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{S/cm}}$ at $50\%$ RH and $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which demonstrated that proton transport pathways within the framework may be discontinuous, and in addition, there is no free acidic group, thus resulting in an ordinary proton conductivity. The lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.3{\ \mathrm{eV}}$ revealed that the protons can conduct by hopping between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units in the pores.
A linear organo-sulfonate (1,4-benzene-dimethane-sulfonate, 1,4-BDMS) and a linear $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -donor $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy as mixed ligands were adopted by Fei group to prepare porous MOFs. Consequently, a 3-D porous MOF, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpy})_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-BDMS})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{0.5}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (TMOF-2), was synthesized with a prototypical primitive-cubic topology [101] which displays a two-fold interpenetration framework with interconnected pore channels having a void space of $37.2\%$ As a comparison, the authors selected a 3D MOF, $\mathsf{I C u}(4,4^{\prime}-$ $\mathsf{b p y})_{2}(\mathsf{E D S})\rfloor_{\mathrm{n}}$ (named TMOF-1) ( $_{\mathrm{Na_{2}E D S}}$ = disodium 1,2- ethanedisulfonate) with the same topological structure prepared in their previous study [102]. The structural difference between the two compounds is that all sulfonate groups in TMOF-1 are involved in coordination with copper atoms, while in TMOF-2, there are uncoordinated sulfonate groups. Both TMOF-2 and TMOF-1 exhibit excellent chemical stability; they can be stable in boiling water, hydrochloric acid solutions $\mathrm{\cdotpH}=2$ or 3), and sodium hydroxide $\mathrm{\DeltapH}=12\$ or 10) solutions for at least a day). The high crystallinity is reserved after above treatment, as evidenced by PXRD patterns. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of TMOF-2 and TMOF-1 are $185\pm3$ and $256\pm5\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. However, the water adsorption amount of TMOF-2 is $\sim20\%$ higher than that of TMOF-1. Accordingly, the highest $\upsigma$ value of TMOF-2 is $1.23\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $98\%$ RH, which is about two orders of magnitude higher than that of TMOF-1 $(8.03\times10^{-6}\ S/\mathrm{cm})$ . This reveals that the uncoordinated $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups in TMOF-2 are easy to capture water molecules and form efficient proton transport channels. In TMOF-1, the sulfonic acid groups all participate in coordination, resulting in limited hydrophilicity, thus the proton conductivity is low. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are $\phantom{-}0.34\mathrm{eV}$ for TMOF-1 and $0.37\mathrm{eV}$ for TMOF-2 meaning that proton conduction belongs to the Grotthuss mechanism.
In 2020, by utilizing 4-{[bis(phosphonomethyl)amino]methyl} benzene-sulfonic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{B P M})$ , Tiemann group reported a 3D barium based MOF, $[\mathrm{Ba}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BPM})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (S-13) [103] in which ${\mathsf{B}}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ cation was coordinated by nine O atoms and located in a monocapped square antiprism. The ${\tt B a O}_{9}$ -polyhedra can be bridged by ${\tt-P O}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}^{-}$ and $-S0_{3}^{-}$ units to construct a microporous 3D framework. Note that ${\tt-P O}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}^{-}$ , $-S0_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{-NH_{4}^{+}}$ groups exist within the framework, which lays a foundation for conduction investigation. More importantly, the authors investigated the proton conduction in this MOF by AC impedance using a single-crystal (SC) sample. The crystal sample was put on top of an electrode array, and then the crystal is therefore easily moved in position and direction relative to the electrode. Thus, they can intuitively study the relationship between proton conductivity and anisotropy. As denoted in Fig. 4a, the long single crystal sample was placed parallel to the electrode, at an angle of $60^{\circ}$ , and perpendicular to the electrode, and then the proton conductivity was measured. The $\upsigma$ of single crystal in the vertical position was an order of magnitude higher than that in the parallel position, and was closer to that in the angle of $60^{\circ}$ . This anisotropy suggests that proton conductivity is an intrinsic property, occurring at least in part inside the crystal lattice and not just on the crystal surface (such as the surfaceadsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ layer). Moreover, for this single crystal, the proton transfer takes place preferentially in the direction of the long crystal. Subsequently, the effects of humidity and temperature on the $\upsigma$ values of the compound were examined. They discovered that at $22{\ }^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ , the $\upsigma$ value of the compound rises exponentially with boosting humidity and temperature. The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value is 0.31 eV for the perpendicular orientation, while for the parallel position $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is $0.54\ \mathrm{eV}$ . This indicates that there are different conductive mechanisms in the two directions of the crystal. In the direction of the long crystal axis, the inherent proton conduction mainly follows the hopping mechanism and is anisotropic, while in the direction perpendicular to the long crystal axis, the proton diffusion mechanism is mainly caused by the adsorption of water molecules on the crystal surface and is isotropic.
Through careful analysis of the crystal structure, the authors found that there were two types of channels along the $c$ -axis (Fig. 4b). One channel was flanked by the phenylene and phosphate groups, whose aperture was too small to accommodate $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units, so it did not help the water-assisted proton conduction. The other was spanned by Ba(II) ions and the sulfonate unit, in which, crystallization water molecules can take shape H-bonds with –NH and phosphate units, which is obviously conducive to the protons hopping.
Although the MOF showed a moderate $\upsigma$ value of $1.23\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $22^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $90\%$ RH, the authors analyzed the conducting mechanism in detail through the test of single crystal sample, which is of great help for people to deeply grasp the conductivie mechanism.
A multi-functional ligand, 4,8-disulfonyl-2,6-naphthalenedicar boxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}T N D C)}$ bearing naphthalene as the skeleton, two –COOH and two $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units as functional groups, can be used as an important precursor for the construction of novel protonconductive MOFs. In 2015, Zhang and coworkers solvothermally prepared three MOFs, 1D $\mathrm{\cdot[Cu(H_{2}T N D C)(D M F)_{4}]_{n}}$ (S-14), 3D [Ca $(\mathrm{TNDC})_{0.5}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2.5}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-15) and 3D $[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{T N D C})_{0.5}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}})$ (S-16) by using this ligand [104]. In MOF S-14, the neighboring $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ atoms are joined by ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TNDC}}^{2-}$ anions to build up a 1D chain, which is further connected to a 2D sheet by $\mathsf{H}$ -bonding interactions. Both MOFs S-15 and S-16 demonstrate 3D frameworks with 1D pores. TG analyses revealed that the crystal structures of S-14 and S-15 hold until $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and S-16 is intact up to $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ After they were humidified (exposed to $95\%$ RH at ambient temperature for 2 days), the PXRD patterns are basically unchanged. The $\upsigma$ values are $3.46\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-14, $1.27\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ for S-15 and $2.49\times10^{-7}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ for S-16 under $95~^{\circ}C$ and $95\%$ RH. By carefully analyzing the crystal structures of these compounds, the reason for their differences in proton conductivity was given. In S-14, H atoms from the two free –COOH units interact with O atoms of sulfonic units and DMF units to form complex and strong hydrogenbond networks on the ab plane, which is intensely helpful for the protons to jump rapidly, resulting in a high conductivity. In S-15 and S-16, all the sulfonic and carboxylic units are full deprotonated and participate in the coordination with metal ions, but the coordination mode is different. Because there are fewer uncoordinated O atoms from sulfonic groups in S-16 than those in S-15, the $\upsigma$ value of S-16 is lower than that of S-15. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of S-14 and S-15 are 0.68 and $0.59\mathrm{eV}$ respectively, ascribing to the Vehicle mechanism. S-16 exhibits a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ $(0.17~\mathrm{eV})$ following a hopping mechanism.
Recently, a novel $S0_{3}\mathrm{H-COOH}$ ligand of 5,7-disulfonate-1,4-na phthalenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}{-}D S N P D C)}$ was designed and prepared by Liu group [105]. By employing this ligand, several LnMOFs, $\{[\mathrm{Ln}_{3}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH})(\mathrm{DSNPDC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{\mathrm{x}}]\cdot\mathrm{yH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (JXNU-7; $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{La}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\textbf{\X}=10,\mathrm{y}=4$ ; $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathbb{N}\mathrm{d}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\mathsf{S m}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathsf{E u}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , $\textbf{\textit{X}}=9$ , $\mathbf{y}=2$ ) and $\{\vert\mathtt{L n}_{4}(\mu_{3^{-}}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{DSNPDC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{11}]{\cdot}28\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (JXNU-8; $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Eu}^{\mathrm{III}}$ , GdIII) are synthesized and characterized. JXNU-7 holds a 3D framework constructed by linear trinuclear Ln-carboxylate subunits. JXNU-8 is a layered structure containing tetranuclear $\mathrm{Ln}_{4}(\mu_{3}–0H)_{4}$ cluster subunits. The Eu analogues of JXNU-7 and $\mathbf{-8}$ exhibit remarkable proton conducting behaviors at high RH related to hydrophilic units in 1-D pores. The proton conductivity of both JXNU-7(Eu) and JXNU8(Eu) shows highly humidity dependent and good reproducibility at different RHs. The $\upsigma$ values of JXNU-7(Eu) and JXNU-8(Eu) are $1.04\times10^{-4}$ and $2.23\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $85~^{\circ}C$ $198\%$ RH, respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values were estimated to be $0.34~\mathrm{eV}$ for JXNU-7(Eu) and $0.18\ \mathrm{eV}$ for JXNU-8(Eu) displaying a Grotthuss proton conducting mechanism. In JXNU-7(Eu), the crystallization $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units inside the pores can interact with O atoms form free sulfonate and carboxylate units and with coordination $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units to constitute 1D ordered H-bonds to offer the fast and efficient proton transmitting channels leading to the high $\upsigma$ value. In JXNU-8(Eu), the crystalline $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units located at the interlayered spaces can take shape Hbonds with the O atoms of free sulfonate units and coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units to offer proton transport channel. Apparently, the $\upsigma$ values of JXNU-8(Eu) are lower than those of JXNU-7(Eu) in the same conditions due to their different structures.
Another similar organic compound, biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -disulfonyl-4, $4^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{BPDSDC})$ having a robust biphenyl backbone and two –COOH and two $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ functional units was synthesized and adopted by Liu group in 2016 to prepare three isomorphous LnMOFs, $\{\mathrm{[LnK(BPDSDC)(DMF)(H_{2}O)]_{x}(s o l v e n t)\}_{\mathrm{n}}}$ (JXNU-2; $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathsf{S}\mathrm{m}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ , and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pr}}^{3+}$ ) [106]. The three 3D MOFs have 1D rod Ln-K subunits that were built by the heterometallic $\operatorname{Ln}(\operatorname{III})$ and $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ atoms joined through the $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ and $-S0_{3}^{-}$ units, and exhibit a Kagome-like lattice with large 1D hexagonal pores (dimensionality: ${\sim}28.2\mathrm{~\AA}$ and minor 1D trigonal pores (dimensionality: ${\sim}13.5\ \mathring\mathrm{A};$ . The proton conductivity of JXNU-2(Sm) shows a dependent on humidity and temperature reaching a high value of
$1.11\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $80~^{\circ}C$ and $98\%$ RH. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ was evaluated to be $0.628\ \mathrm{eV}$ , belonging to a vehicle proton conduction mechanism. Note that the free sulfonate oxygen O10, and O4 and O5 atoms on the framework, which are not involved in coordination, protrude into the trigonal and the hexagonal channels, respectively. This will greatly increase the hydrophilicity of the pores, thus facilitating the adsorption of the solvate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units in the pores to form hydrated protons for diffusion resulting in a high conductivity.
Compared with $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -BPDSDC, $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -disulfonyl- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicar boxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{4}D B D C)}$ has the same backbone and functional groups, but the spatial position of sulfonic functional group is different. By using $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DBDC}$ ligand, Zhu et al. made up a series of 3D isomorphous and isostructural LnMOFs $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{LnDBDC(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}]$ $(\mathrm{Ln}\ =\ \mathrm{Eu}$ , Gd, Tb, Dy) in 2017 [107]. All MOFs exhibit anionic three-dimensional frameworks with bnn topology built by Ln-OC rods with 1D pores filled $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ cations, consisting of a particular hepta-coordinated $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ion and two coordination sulfonic groups. The experimental patterns of treated samples (dipping in water for 3 months and in $\mathsf{p H}=4-12$ aqueous solutions for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ ) are close to the simulated ones, denoting that the LnMOFs materials have a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ stability and chemical stability. Eu-MOF emerges low $\upsigma$ of $4.14\times10^{-8}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under $95\%$ RH and $25^{\circ}C$ , possibly due to inefficient proton transport pathways through hydrogen bond networks within small 1D pores. Lately, Zhu’s research group synthesized a 3D copper MOF $\mathrm{\{[Cu_{2}(D B D C)(4,4^{\prime}-}}$ $\mathbf{bpy})_{2.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})\mathrm{I}{\cdot}1{\cdot}.7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}};$ with $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{DBDC}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy mixed ligands [108]. This MOF can preserve its structural rigidity and crystallinity even after soaking in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ for 10 days, in boiling $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for 1 day, and also indicates a low $\upsigma$ of $4.48\times10^{-7}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ with a high $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{a}}^{\mathsf{}}\left(0.95\mathsf{e V}\right)$ at $95\%$ RH and $65^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The low $\upsigma$ of this MOF may be ascribing to the low $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ concentration and an inefficient proton transferring pathway.
Disodium- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -disulfonate- $\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}$ -oxydibenzoic acid $(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\cdot$ DSOA) as a monomer is appropriate for synthesis of sulfonated MOFs. $\mathsf{D S O A}^{4-}$ anion has two aromatic carboxylates at the terminal and two sulfonates in the middle, which is flexible and easy to construct a porous hydrophilic network. Therefore, Dong group reported a 3D MOF, $\{\mathrm{[H_{3}O][C u_{2}(D S O A)(O H)(H_{2}O)]}}.9.5\mathrm{H_{2}O\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ by utilizing $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{S}0\mathsf{A}$ ligand [109] (Cu-DSOA) with two similar large hydrophilic pores lined with the uncoordinated oxygen atoms of sulfonic groups and filled with $\left(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{O}\right)^{+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units (Fig. 5a and 5b). Cu-DSOA exhibited a high $\upsigma$ of $1.9\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ $E_{\mathrm{a}}=1.04~\mathrm{eV};$ at $85~^{\circ}C$ and $98\%$ RH. The experimental results indicate that delocalized $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ enhance their directional mobility over long ranges with an increase in the temperature, by which protons can be transported by the vehicle mechanism.
Subsequently, the same team prepared a 3D porous Tb(III) MOF, $\{\mathrm{[Tb_{4}(O H)_{4}(D S O A)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{8}]\cdot(H_{2}O)_{8}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (Tb-DSOA) with 1D open hydrophilic pores filled with non-coordinated O atoms of sulfonic groups (Fig. 5c and 5d) [110]. This MOF evidences structural stability after immersion in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for 28 days at room temperature or in boiling $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for 1 day as evidenced by the PXRD determinations. The AC impedance measurements of the pellet samples manifest that below $68\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ values vary from $10^{-7}$ to $10^{-9}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ which were not highly dependent on RH and temperature. Above $68\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ values increase rapidly with the increase of RH and temperature. The highest $\upsigma$ value of $1.66\times10^{-4}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $98\%$ RH can be acquired. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is evaluated to be $0.45\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $98\%$ RH. A Grotthuss-type proton transfer mechanism can be observed. Meanwhile, in the above proton conduction process, the vehicle mechanism may also be involved, especially under high humidity. Cu-DSOA and Tb-DSOA possess 3D structures with similar tetranuclear $\mathrm{[Cu_{4}(\upmu_{3}-O H)_{2}]^{6+}}$ and $\mathrm{[Tb_{4}(\mu_{3}–O H)_{4}]^{8+}}$ SUBs, nevertheless, their proton conductivity is different, which may be due to the different coordination modes of sulfonate units in the two structures.
In 2018, Konar and coworkers synthesized a series of 3D LnMOFs $(\{[\mathrm{Ln}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{DSOA})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}]\cdot4\cdot.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot1.4\mathrm{MeCN}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Gd}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (S-17), DyIII(S-18), $\mathrm{Ho}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (S-19) and $\mathrm{Er}^{\mathrm{III}}$ (S-20))) using $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{S}0\mathsf{A}$ [111]. The procured LnMOFs hold $\mathrm{[Ln_{4}(}\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH})_{4}]^{8+}$ cubane-like cores that are joined by ${\mathrm{L}}^{4-}$ anions to construct the 3D networks with one-dimensional hydrophilic pores along the c-axis. The $\upsigma$ values of all the MOFs exhibit a dependence on temperature and humidity, and obtained the highest values of $2.02\times10^{-6}S/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-17, $2.96\times10^{-6}S/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-18, $4.56\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-19 and $6.59\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathsf{S/c m}$ for S-20 at $80~\%$ RH. Notably, the $\upsigma$ values of S-19 and S-20 are about three orders of magnitude higher than those of S-17 and S-18, which are attributed to the increase of acidity of coordination $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules with the enhancing of cation charge density, that is, the decreasing order of ionic radius $(\mathrm{Gd^{III}}>\mathrm{Dy^{III}}>\mathrm{Ho^{III}}>\mathrm{Er^{III}},$ . Water vapor adsorption data appear that for S-19 and S-20, each metal adsorbs $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ molecules, while for S-17 and S-18, each metal atom adsorbs $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules, which is also an important reason for the higher conductivity of S-19 and S-20 than that of S-17 and S-18. For LnMOFs S17-20, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are in the range of $0.27{-}0.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ suggesting that proton conduction was operated by a Grotthuss mechanism. The authors believed that the lanthanide contraction strategy could be used to adjust the proton conductivities of the relevant MOFs.
The flexible ligand, 1,2-bis(sodium-2-sulfonate-4-carboxyphe noxy)ethane $\left(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{L}\right)$ includes two sulfonate groups and two – COOH groups, which can be adopted to build up various MOFs. Subsequently, Zang et al. reported a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based three-dimensional MOF $\mathrm{[Cp_{3}Z r_{3}(\mu_{3}-O)(\mu_{3}-O H)_{3}]_{2}L_{3}.4N a\cdot H_{2}O}$ (MOP-1) for proton conductive study [112] which holds candy-shaped porous structure. Note that the $-S0_{3}^{-}$ groups can interact with $(\eta^{5}–C_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{3}Z\mathrm{r}_{3}(\mu_{3}–0)$ $(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-}}0\mathrm{H})_{3}$ to form charge assisted H-bonds. Besides, 2D H-bonded network can be constructed by the connection of coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and crystalline $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units. These special H-bonds of the structure enhance its water stability and chemical stability. The TGA determination exhibits that MOP-1 maintains thermal stability at temperatures up to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The PXRD patterns of the samples treated with acid and alkali solutions $\mathrm{PH}=3$ , 7, 9, 11, 1 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , 2 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4},$ ) have no obvious change displaying its strong structural rigidity. The proton conductivity of MOP-1 shows a significant dependence on temperature and humidity. In particular, at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the conductivity of MOP-1 is relatively low and shows a trend of slow increase (from $6.28\times10^{-6}$ to $6.54\times10^{-5}\ S/\mathrm{cm})$ with the increase of humidity in the range of $22\%-73\%$ RHs. Nevertheless, from $93\%$ to $98\%$ RHs, the conductivity showed a rapid increase from $2.28\times10^{-4}$ to $1.41\times10^{-3}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ . This phenomenon manifests that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units act as a critical role in proton conduction. $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ was intensively explored and obtained a low value of $0.225{\mathrm{~eV}}$ . Obviously, abundant H-bonded networks formed by $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , coordinated water units and adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units are very helpful for the protons hopping.
Both 2-(2,4-disulfophenyl)imidazo(4,5-f)(1,10)-phenanthroline (Hsfpip) and 2-sulfophenylimidazo(4,5-f)(1,10)-phenanthroline $({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{s p i p}})$ hold rigid o-phenanthroline plane and flexible $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units, and can be used as bridging and chelating ligands. Moreover, when $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ is very low, sulfonic groups are easy to dissociate, resulting in different coordination modes. Therefore, corresponding ligand with free sulfonate groups can be controlled and adjusted as proton-conduction carriers. In 2017, Su group employed Hsfpip [113] ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{s p i p}}$ [114] synthesized four MOFs, 2D $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu(Hsfpip)(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (S-21), 1D $[\mathrm{CuH(Hsfpip)Cl(H_{2}O)}]$ (S-22), 1D $\mathrm{Cu_{2}H_{2}(H s p i p)_{2}C l_{4}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (S-23) and 2D $\mathrm{CuH(Hspip)(HPO_{4})}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ (S-24) via HCl steam-assisted conversion approach. The relationship between proton-conducting property and structural characteristic is explored. The highest $\upsigma$ values are $1.43\times10^{-3}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-21, $1.50\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ for S-22, $6.47\times10^{-3}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for S-23 and $6.90\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ for S-24 at $95~^{\circ}C$ and $97\%$ RH. The $\upsigma$ values are obviously arranged in the order of $\pmb{S}\mathbf{-}\pmb{21}<\pmb{S}\mathbf{-}\pmb{22}$ and $S-24<S-23$ which are attributed to the fact that noncoordinated $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ can increase the $\upsigma$ value, and the halide Cl ions and $-S0_{3}^{-}$ groups between neighboring chains may take shape hydrophilic channels and stacking methods to improve the $\upsigma$ values. Moreover, the HCl steam-assisted conversion approach may preserve sulfonic acid groups from coordinating with metals and protonate the $-S0_{3}^{-}$ unit, thereby increasing proton conductivity. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of S21 and S-22 at $85\%$ RH were $0.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and $0.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The $E_{\mathbf{a}}$ for S-23 was estimated to be 1.05 eV $(25-60\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ and $0.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(60-95~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ for S-24 was 0.68 eV $(25-60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and 1.76 eV $(60-95~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . Obviously, according to the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values, the proton conduction mechanism can be easily judged $_{<0.4\mathrm{eV}}$ belong to the Grotthuss mechanism; greater than $0.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to the vehicle mechanism). Although the authors have determined the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and thermal stability of these MOFs, unfortunately, they have not tested their water stability.
## 2.1.2. MOFs bearing uncoordinated sulfonic acid groups
To date, only a few reports upon MOFs contain protonated $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. The MOFs with protonated $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups improve the number of carriers, thus enhancing their inherent proton conductivity.
In 2014, three porous $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ sulfonate MOFs, $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}5-\mathrm{sip})(4,4^{\prime}-$ bpy)] DMF $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{1})$ , $[\mathrm{Zn(H_{2}O)(H5-S i p)-(b p e l)_{0.5}}]$ DMF $(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{}\mathbf{2})$ , $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})_{2}(\mathrm{H}5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpy})]$ (DMF)2(DMA) $(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{3})$ (bpel $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ trans1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene; $\mathrm{DMA}=(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})^{+})$ with free $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, $-S0_{3}^{-}$ and DMA cations were solvothermally synthesized by Kitagawa group (Fig. 6) [115]. Zn-1 reveals a 2D layered structure bearing 1D pore (size: $5.6\times7.1\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2},$ . Zn-2 has a 1D ladderchain architecture with 1D pores (size: $10.4\times3.1\mathrm{~\AA^{2}}.$ . Zn-3 is a 3D structure with 1D narrow pores (size: $3.4\times4.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}.$ , and possesses an ordered proton source of 5-sip on the pore surface. Note that in all the frameworks of the three MOFs, $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups are located at the pore surface and are lined along the channel direction, which suggests that these compounds will be good proton conductors. The further determinations reveal that Zn-1 exhibits a higher $\upsigma$ of $3.9\times10^{-4}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $25^{\circ}C$ and $60\%$ RH, and $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{2}$ demonstrates a lower $\upsigma$ of $3.4\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under the identical conditions because of lack of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units. Although Zn-3 has two proton sources of DMA cations and $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, Zn-3 indicates a moderate $\upsigma$ of $8.7\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ . This result shows that the small pores of $\mathbf{Z_{n-3}}$ are not conducive to proton conduction, resulting in low conductivity. Nevertheless, at such low humidity, the free sulfonic group in the MOFs still plays a vital role in proton transport.
In 2019, Choe group reported the interesting hybrid solid conductive materials, UMOM-100-a and UMOM-100-b, forged through incorporation of Cu-based metal organic polyhedra (MOPs) into a porous MOF host [116]. PCN-777 [117] (robust porosity $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF) and $\mathrm{Na_{6}H_{18}}[\mathrm{Cu_{24}}(\mathrm{SO_{3}^{-}}\mathrm{-mBDC})_{24}(\mathrm{G})_{24}]$ (MOP 3) (G representing a coordination solvent) [118] are prepared by the reference methods as the hosts and guests, respectively. PCN-777 is a $\beta$ -cristobalite-type framework and involves two kinds of cages ( $15\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ tetrahedron and $38\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ truncated tetrahedron). Notably, the 38 Å cage drives PCN-777 a proper host for loading other components. MOP 3 bears twelve $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{C O O})_{4}$ paddlewheel cores and twentyfour 5-sulfoisophthalic acid units ( $S0_{3}^{-}$ -mBDC), and is a appropriate guest due to its size and solubility of cubic-octahedral cages. Moreover, the MOP guest has $-S0_{3}^{-}$ groups, which act as a functional nanocage, while the porosity still maintains for proton conductivity. Under $80^{\circ}C$ and $90\%$ RH, the conductivities are $1.40\times10^{-4}\:\mathsf{S}/$ cm for UMOM-100-a and $2.11\times10^{-4}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for UMOM-100-b. The $\upsigma$ values of the hybrid materials are 7 orders of magnitude greater than that (about $10^{-11}\mathrm{S/cm})$ under $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $30\%$ RH, and 4 orders of magnitude greater than that of PCN-777 (about $10^{-8}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ , at $25\ ^{\circ}C$ and $30\%$ RH). The higher $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of UMOM-100-a, UMOM-100-b and PCN-777 are 0.78, 0.66, and $0.87\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively, which are because of the irregular MOP cage position, and the protonic species facilitate the diffusion protons with synsemantic hydrogen-bonds. The experimental results illustrate that the sulfonic acid groups in MOFs can effectively provide protons and constitute a rich H-bonded network with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units, and play an important role for the proton conductor under humidifying conditions.
A tactical method is to modify MOF with Brønsted acid-base pairs, which can build up intermolecular H-bonds to establish proton-conduction tracers. Based on this, in 2017, Shimizu group reported a sulfonated In-based MOF $\{[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{H}5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})_{2}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})]$ DMF $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{1.4}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (PCMOF-17) by adopting $\mathrm{H}_{3}5$ -sip with a high proton conductivity and modest water stability [119]. PCMOF-17 possesses a two-dimensional anion layer structure with 1D pores, which combines H-bonding ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ cations and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units (Fig. 7). PCMOF-17 was unstable under high humidity and became amorphous in greater than $60\%$ RH. The bulk PCMOF-17 emerged that the proton conduction value reached $1.17\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under $25\ \%$ RH. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is evaluated to be 0.31 eV showing the Grotthuss-type mechanism. As the platinum electrodes were attached to the $\{-110\}$ , {110}, and {001} crystallographic faces of the single crystal sample of PCMOF-17, the determined $\upsigma$ values are $1.25\times10^{-3}$ , $1.20\times10^{-3}$ , and $8.66\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{S/cm}(25^{\circ}C,40\%R H),}$ respectively. The values of $\{-110\}$ and {110} faces are comparable to the value obtained with powder samples, because a monolithic system along the prior axis is expected to achieve higher conductivity compared to casually oriented powders with more grain boundaries. The continuous two-dimensional H-bonded network is demonstrated in Fig. 7c makes it possible to conduct among these faces. However, the $\upsigma$ along the $c\mathrm{.}$ -axis is smaller than that of the other two directions. Additionally, the triaxial conductivity tests demonstrate the major proton-transfer paths in PCMOF-17 are associated with the rich H-bonds paths and independent of inherent pore structures.
In 2019, by the self-assembly reaction of isostructural auxiliary ligands bearing different acidic units with a calix[4]resorcinarene ligand (TPC4R-I) and ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ salt, Ma et al. prepared two proton conductive metal–organic cages (MOCs), $[\mathsf{C o}_{16}(\mathrm{TPC}4\mathsf{R}-\mathrm{I})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}5-$ sip $))_{4}(\mathrm{H}5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sip})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{24}]\cdot12\mathrm{DMF}\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\mathbf{(1-SO_{3}H)}$ and $[\mathsf{C o}_{16}(\mathsf{T P C4R-}$ $\mathrm{I)_{2}(H_{2}5-h i p)_{4}(H5-h i p)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{16}]\cdot17D M A\cdot H_{2}C}$ (1-OH) $(\mathrm{H}_{3}5{\cdot}\mathrm{hip}=5{\cdot}$ hydroxy-isophthalic acid) [120]. The two MOCs are isostructural and contain two TPC4R-I ligands, 12 isophthalic acid derivatives and $16~\mathsf{C o}(\mathrm{II})$ ions featuring $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{16}\right]$ cage frameworks. The sizes of the internal cavities and the entire cages are about 2.3 and $2.7~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. The one-dimensional channel is filled with H-bonds constituted by $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}/{\mathrm{OH}}$ groups and guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ units, which provides an efficient pathway for proton transfer. Consequently, the proton conductivity was evaluated by AC determinations. At $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $80\text{\textperthousand}$ RHs, the $\upsigma$ values of $\mathbf{1-SO_{3}H}$ drastically boosted with the rising RH $(1.68\times10^{-7}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $80\%$ RH; $5.75\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $98\%$ RH). In contrast, 1-OH kept a minor $\upsigma$ value of $7.03\times10^{-6}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ until RH is equal to $98\%$ . It can be found that under the same test conditions, the proton conductivity of $\mathbf{1-SO_{3}H}$ is much higher than that of 1-OH, which indicates that the introduction of sulfonate groups can greatly improve the proton conductivity. At $98\%$ RH, their $\upsigma$ values enhanced rapidly with the elevating temperature from 25 to $90^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The highest $\upsigma$ values of $1.35\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for $\mathbf{1-SO_{3}H}$ and $5.10\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S/cm}$ for 1-OH under $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $98\%$ RH can be observed. The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values for 1- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ were 0.295 eV $(25-40\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf C})$ and 1.316 eV $(50-90^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ at $98\%\mathrm{RH}$ , respectively. For 1-OH, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are in the range of 0.414– $1.603\mathrm{eV}$ , which are higher than those of $\mathbf{1-SO_{3}H}.$ This is consistent with the proton conductivity test results of the two compounds.
Both compounds have higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ uptakes of 729.94 and $613.37~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, which also suggests that they can adsorb water molecules to form a rich H-bond network for proton transport. In one word, the weak $\mathsf{H}$ -bonds between the sulfonic group and water are conducive to offering protons. In addition, the H-bonded networks formed by free $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $S0_{3}^{-}$ and adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units lead to the high proton conductivity of $\mathbf{1-SO_{3}H}$ .
Proton mobility is the main factor that causes $\upsigma$ to soar with the increase of defect concentration. Ligand vacancies are a kind of defect, which could adjust the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of metal-clusters to affect the $\upsigma$ value of MOFs. In 2015, Taylor group reported an acid functionalized MOF $(\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{8}L_{4.2}{\cdot}x H_{2}O})$ using 2–2-sulfonterephthalica cid $\left(\mathrm{BDC-SO_{3}H}\right).$ to introduce ligand vacancies [121]. The MOF included a lot of partially occupied ligands and $Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IIII})$ ions which affected the porosity and $\upsigma$ . The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF has a mass of strongly acidic $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups and large, open, hydrated, 3D interconnected channels. Additionally, ligand defects promoted charge transfer because the channels were more open. The DFT calculations revealed that the released protons were trapped in the defective clusters to take shape a stable framework. The $\upmu_{2}$ -oxide in the defect including a cluster acted as proton trapping sites $(\mathrm{d}{-}Z\Gamma{\mathrm{OH}})$ because the calculated $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of 13.3 was similar to alcohol. To prove the hypothesis, the $\upsigma$ of the material after acid treatment was increased by adding excessive secondary acid. Through synthesis in the presence of sulfoaceticacid, the conductivity was increased 5 times, attaining a maximum value of $5.62\ \times\ 10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ $(65~\%)$ RH). Experimental data revealed that defect sites in MOFs significantly changed the surface properties of MOFs, resulting in a dramatic change for the proton capture properties.
In the same year, Li and coworkers presented another $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based MOF $([Z\Gamma_{6}0_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC-SO_{3}H})_{6}]\cdot1.5(\mathrm{BDC-SO_{3}H}))$ $\mathbf{\left(UiO-66-5O_{3}H\right)}$ by employing BDC- $S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ ligand (Fig. 8) [122] in which each $Z r_{6}O_{4}(-$ $\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{12}$ cluster is connected by twelve terephthalicacid units, forming centric octahedron encircled by 8 minor tetrahedral-cages. The cages are bridged to each other through shared trigonal windows. These structural characteristics of UiO-66- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{so_{3}H}$ make its excellent thermal-stability and super-high resistance to some chemicals and external pressures. Interestingly, several analogues, UiO66-2COOH, UiO66- ${\bf N H}_{2}$ and UiO66-Br bearing different functional units (Fig. 8a) were simultaneously prepared for the comparative study of proton conductivity. The results illustrated that UiO$\mathbf{66-SO_{3}H}$ with one free $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ group or UiO66-2COOH with two free –COOH groups exhibited high proton conductivity, reaching more than $10^{-2}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ under $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\sim97\%$ RH. In contrast, UiO-66, UiO66- ${\bf N H}_{2}$ and UiO66-Br indicated low proton conductivity under the identical conditions. This indicates that the introduction of acidic free $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups and –COOH groups will lead to a high hydrophilicity of the related MOFs, which is conducive to the formation of hydrogen bonds within the framework and ultimately leads to the high proton conductivity. Consequently, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values were calculated to be 0.27, 0.18, 0.40, 0.44 and $0.78\ \mathrm{eV}$ for UiO66- $\mathbf{\delta_{S}0_{3}H}$ , UiO66-2COOH, UiO66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ , UiO-66 and UiO66- Br, respectively. Such an increasing order of $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values indicates that proton transport in these MOFs proceeds according to the Grotthuss mechanism to the vehicle mechanism, which is consistent with the changing trend of the proton conductivity of these MOFs. The authors further confirmed that these MOFs could keep their intact after AC determinations for one week. Moreover, they adopted the molecular simulation manner to theoretically reveal the formation of H-bonded network in these MOFs.
Metalorganic gel (MOG) is a new type of short-range wellorganized coordination polymers with a good 3D osmotic network. In 2020, Jiang group prepared a 3D Cr-based nano-structured MOG (namely Cr/sBDC-Gel) by a facile sol–gel approach employing BDC$S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ as starting metarials [123]. The integrated 3D gelnetwork provides a long distance orderly proton transfer pores built by sulfonate units, which promotes the rapid transmission of protons along the well-interconnected $\mathsf{H}$ -bonded networks, and reduced the energy barrier for the proton transfer. Hence, the $\upsigma$ values of Cr/sBDC-Gels improved with precursor concentration $(0.2\substack{-0.4\mathrm{~M}})$ . The $\upsigma$ values of $\mathbf{Cr}/s\mathbf{BDC-Gel-0.2}$ and $\mathbf{Cr/sBDC-}$ Gel-0.4 are $5.0\times10^{-3}$ and $7.8\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ under $80~\ {^\circ}C/100\%$ RH, respectively, which are greater than that of the original CrMOF $(8.7\times10^{-4}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ . The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of $\mathbf{Cr/sBDC-Gel-0.2\M}$ and Cr/sBDC-Gel-0.4 are 0.34 and $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, which manifest the Grotthuss-like transport behavior. The integration of sulfonated compounds into the gel-framework enriched the structural multiplicity of MOGs and provided novel viewpoint for design of new proton conductive material.
In 2017, by utilizing 4-sulfonaphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}S N D C)}$ , Tu et al. prepared a 3D $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF $([Z\mathrm{r}_{6}0_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{8}(-$ ${\mathsf{H S N D C}})_{4}$ , VNU-17) [124] which adopts the bcu topology and contains the dense $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units lining $6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ channels. Then, VNU-17 is used as the platform to incorporate imidazole $\left(\operatorname{Him}\right)$ with different loadings into the internal void space, resulting in $\mathbf{HIm_{9}}\subset\mathbf{VNU-17}$ and $\bf{H I m}_{11}\subset\bf{V N U-17\cdot H}_{2}O$ sorption experiments demonstrated that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptakes for VNU17, $\mathbf{HIm_{9}}\subset\mathbf{VNU-17}$ and $\mathbf{HIm_{11}}-$ $\subset$ VNU-17 are 304, 256 and $320~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ as $P/P_{0}=0.85$ , respectively indicating that the pores of the MOFs were blocked by Him units. However, the pores of the post-modified MOFs are filled with dense Him units as well as $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units and exhibit high water adsorption capacity, which will lay the foundation for efficient proton transport. At $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $85\%$ RH, along with the increasing loadings of HIm, the $\upsigma$ values of $\mathbf{HIm_{9}}\subset\mathbf{VNU-17}$ $(1.53\times10^{-4}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ and HIm11 VNU-17 $(5.93\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ rapidly increase. Notably, the $\upsigma$ value of $\mathbf{HIm_{11}}\subset\mathbf{VNU-17}$ is 900-fold enhancement in comparison with the parent VNU-17 $(6.65\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ under $98\%$ RH and $70~^{\circ}C$ and maintains its stability for more than $40\mathrm{~h~}$ The obtained $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value for $\bf{H I m}_{11}\subset V N U-17$ is $0.27\ \mathrm{eV}$ revealing that the proton transport obeys the Grotthuss mechanism, which may be attributed to the high polarity of imidazole ions to completely hydrate the entire pores and grain boundaries. Apparently, the post-synthesis load HIm formed Brønsted acid-base pairs in MOFs can enhance the proton conductivity.
Compared with $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{S N D C}$ ligand, 4,8-disulfonyl-2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{4}T N D C)}$ can provide one more $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ group. In 2018, Tu group fabricated a similar $Z\mathrm{r}^{\mathrm{IV}}.$ -based MOF, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}0_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{8}(-$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TNDC)}_{4}]$ (VNU-23) using ${\tt H}_{4}\mathrm{TNDC}$ [125]. VNU-23 has similar structural characteristics with VNU-17. The authors utilized the same method to anchor organic small histamine (His) molecules into VNU-23 to enhance its $\upsigma$ values. The obtained MOF of $\bf H i s_{8.2^{-}}$ $\mathbf{\mathcal{C}\Psi\Psi\Psi11}\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{\Psi}\mathbf{\Psi}$ displayed a high $\upsigma$ of $1.79\times10^{-2}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $98\%$ RH and $95~^{\circ}C$ , and the value can be kept for more than $120\mathrm{~h~}$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value was $0.27\ \mathrm{eV}$ suggesting the same conducting mechanism as VNU-17.
In 2017, Chen et al. made a highly conductive flexible MOF, $(\mathrm{Cr_{3}}(\mu_{3}{-}0)(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{3}(\mathrm{TNDC)_{3}(D M A)_{3}}$ (BUT-8(Cr)) [126] in which each $\operatorname{Cr}_{3}(\mu_{3}–0)$ cluster is connected to 6 organic-ligands to take shape a 3D framework with 1D pores along the $c\mathrm{.}$ -axis, and the bipyramidal cages (diameters: ${\sim}12$ and $14\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Fig. 9). Notably, a highdensity of $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ sites are decorated on pore surfaces for proton transfer. Through exchange with sulphuric-acid aqueous solution, the counter-ions $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{2}^{+})$ are replaced by $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ to get BUT-8 $(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{A}$ . BUT- ${\mathbf{8(Cr)A}}$ was stable in various $\mathsf{p H}$ aqueous solutions, even in 10 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ or concentrated HCl for 7 days. The PXRD researches demonstrated that after exposure of the newly synthesized samples (BUT- ${\pmb8}({\bf C r}){\pmb{\mathrm{A}}})$ to $30\%$ RH for about forty minutes, the diffraction peaks broaden and shift, and the color of the samples also changes from green to light green. Dry BUT- ${\pmb{\mathscr{s}}}({\pmb{\mathscr{c}}}{\pmb{\mathrm{r}}}){\pmb{\mathscr{A}}}$ has poor crystallinity and broad diffraction peaks, but when the dried samples are processed in humid air, the PXRD patterns and color can be quickly restored in ${<}2\ \mathrm{min}$ . Due to the inherent characteristics of
MOF, the structure changed under different %RH depended on water content. The increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ content with rising $\%\mathrm{RH}$ apparently improves the $\upsigma$ values implying that proton mainly migrates across the inner pores. Specifically, both BUT- ${\mathbf{8(Cr)}}{\mathbf{A}}$ and BUT-8 $(\mathbf{Cr})$ demonstrate a lower $\upsigma$ values of $4.19\times10^{-6}$ and $5.75\times10^{-7}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{cm}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ and $11\%$ RH, respectively. Then the values can be enhanced to $1.12\times10^{-3}$ and $6.32\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S/cm}$ as RH being $65\%$ , respectively. Finally, as RH being equal to $100\%$ RH, their $\upsigma$ values boosted to $7.61\times10^{-2}$ and $1.50\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ , respectively. Such high $\upsigma$ values can be maintained even over a wide RH range, probably due to the flexibility and ‘‘self-adaptation” nature of the framework. The $\upsigma$ values for BUT- ${\pmb8}({\bf c r}){\pmb A}$ and BUT-8(Cr) were further studied at elevated temperature and humidity $(80~\mathrm{^circC}\mathrm{-}100\%$ RH). BUT- ${\pmb8}({\bf c r}){\pmb A}$ and BUT- ${\mathbf{8(Cr)}}$ demonstrate increased $\upsigma$ of $4.63\times10^{-2}$ and $1.27\times10^{-1}\:\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ , respectively. The evaluated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are $0.11\ \mathrm{eV}$ for BUT- ${\mathbf{\cdot8(cr)}}{\mathbf{A}}$ and $0.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ for BUT-8(Cr). The high $\upsigma$ and low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of BUT- ${\pmb8}({\bf c r}){\bf A}$ and BUT-8(Cr) confirmed fastion conducting behaviours, which are a reasonable explanation based on the Grotthus-type mechanism. The high carrier density and good structural stability obtained through cation exchange are beneficial to improving proton conductivity.
In addition to containing carboxylate functionality, sulfone biphenyldicarboxylic acid $[\mathsf{H}_{2}s\mathsf{b}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{c})$ also has similar sulfone function, which makes $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{s b d c}$ a worthy candidate for designing protonconducting MOFs. Thus, Yamada group reported a 1D Zn-based MOF $([\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{sbdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}].(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sbdc}).\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ bearing a sulfone group [127]. In this MOF, each sbdc2 anion bridged neighboring $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ atoms to build up an infinite chain. Notably, the uncoordinated $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ - sbdc ligands are sandwiched by these chains. Therefore, the Hbonded network inside the framework can be constructed by the coordinated and free $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ units and organic ligands, which is beneficial to proton conduction. The $\upsigma$ values of this MOF were determined by AC impedance and depended on the $\%\mathrm{RH}$ ( $30\%$ RH: $4.1\times10^{-13}\mathsf{S}/$ cm; $95\%$ RH: $2.5\times10^{-7}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{cm}}{}_{.}$ . The lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.32\mathrm{eV}$ denoted that the protons hop through hydrogen-bonding network. Although the proton conductivity of the MOF is relatively minor, it can increase significantly with the increase of humidity, which indicates that the MOF can be further studied as a humidity sensor.
In 2012, Banerjee group used a similar $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -sulfobisbenzoic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}S\mathrm{B}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{A)}$ ligand having two –COOH groups and a functional sulfone group to $\mathsf{H}_{2}s\mathsf{b}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{c}$ to prepare three 2D alkaline-earth metal MOFs (Ca-SBBA, Sr-SBBA and Ba-SBBA) [128]. Although the three MOFs bear the same ligands and the same Group II metals, their structural characteristics were totally different, which may be due to the metal coordination number, different binding methods and different bending angles of $\mathsf{S B B A}^{2-}$ ligands. All the three MOFs illustrated high thermal stability up to $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and possessed a crystalline phase change ${\bf\tau}>100{\bf\tau}^{\circ}{\bf C}.$ Both Ca-SBBA and Sr-SBBA maintained their structural stability after placed under $98\%$ RH for $1-$ 2 days, which is confirmed by PXRD measurements. Nevertheless, Ba-SBBA lost its crystallinity and phase purity at humidified conditions. Accordingly, their proton conductive behaviors are different. Both Ca-SBBA and Sr-SBBA displayed ordinary $\upsigma$ values of $8.6\times10^{-6}$ and $4.4\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, respectively, and were highly humidity-dependent. As RH being $60\%$ RH, the values decreased to $2.87\times10^{-7}$ and $3.47\times10^{-6}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{cm}}$ , respectively. In contrast, Ba-SBBA did not exhibit proton conductive behavior under similar conditions, which may be due to its low stability under humidity conditions. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are calculated to be $0.23\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathbf{c_{a}}$ -SBBA and 0.56 eV for Sr-SBBA, implying that proton conduction in the former follows the Grotthuss mechanism and in the latter obeys the vehicle mechanism. The $\upsigma$ value of Sr-SBBA is higher than that of Ca-SBBA meaning that the carrier concentration in the former is higher. Obviously, the three MOFs exhibited different proton conduction behavior due to structural-variation and physic-chemical properties.
In 2014, Natarajan et al. reported two Mn-based MOFs, $\left[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right]$ $[\mathsf{M n}_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{S B B A})_{3}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})](\mathsf{D M F})_{5}$ (Mn-1), and $[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]_{2}[\mathrm{Mn}_{7}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{OH)_{4}(S B B A)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{4}](H_{2}O)_{2}(D M F)_{8}}$ (Mn-2) by adopting $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S B}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{A}$ via solvothermal methods [129]. Note that in the process of preparation, a marginal difference in the initial pH value (Mn-1: $\mathsf{p H}=1.3$ ; Mn-2: $\mathsf{p H}=2.2\AA$ ) leads to the great difference in the subsequent structures, which provides beneficial inspiration for us to control the synthetic MOFs. Both MOFs possess a 3D structure constructed by $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{6}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}_{7}}$ clusters with 1D pores lined by $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ ions and solvent molecules, and keep their structural rigidity after $24\mathrm{h}$ of exposure at $98\%$ RH. The proton conductivity of both MOFs boosted with the increase RH. At $34^{\circ}C$ and $98\%$ RH, their $\upsigma$ values are around $3.0\times10^{-4}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ . The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ are estimated to be 0.93 and $1.16\ \mathrm{eV}$ , for Mn-1 and Mn-2, respectively, following the vehicle proton conducting mechanism. The $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ cations lined in the pores may be served as the vehicle to deliver protons.
Ligand $p$ -sulfonatocalix[4]arene (TSTT) has four sulfonate groups and four –OH groups, which can not only connect metals into multi-dimensional frameworks but also serve as proton carriers. In 2013, Zhang et al. prepared a 3D Cu-based MOF (Cu-TSTT) via molecule recognition, in which continuous $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules are located inside the channels [130]. The MOF denoted a low $\upsigma$ of $5.42\times10^{-7}\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH. Its $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $0.39\mathrm{eV}$ indicates a Grotthuss-type mechanism $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules, $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups and $-\mathsf{O H}$ groups form H-bonded networks to provide proton conduction channels.
In 2014, Ghosh et al. fabricated an interesting 3D MOF, $\{(\mathrm{DMA})_{3}(\mathrm{SO_{4}})]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\langle0\mathbf{{X}}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ oxalate) [131] in which $\vert Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}\vert_{\mathrm{n}}^{2-}$ anions were interpenetrated with $[(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{4}]_{\mathrm{n}}^{+}$ cations that were built by electrostatic and $\mathsf{H}$ -bonds interactions between $S0_{4}^{2-}$ units and DMA cations (Fig. 10). The $\vert Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}\vert_{\mathrm{n}}^{2-}$ network contains three-dimensional channels filled with DMA cations and disordered $S0_{4}^{2-}$ anions (Fig. 10c). TGA revealed that this MOF maintained its thermal stability up to $180^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Moreover, it can keep its water stability after soaked in $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for one day as affirmed by PXRD patterns. First, the authors found that this MOF had a high $\upsigma$ of $1.0\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Also, this $\upsigma$ value can keep for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a negligible loss showing the durability of this MOF. The calculated anhydrous $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is $0.13\mathrm{eV}$ being attributed to the Grotthuss proton conduction mechanism. Obviously, this kind of high efficiency proton hopping mainly occurs in the dense H-bonded network formed by DMA cations and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ anions in the pores. Second, the water-assisted $\upsigma$ value of this MOF was explored under ambient temperature and various RHs. With the increase of humidity, the $\upsigma$ also soars from $4.4\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{S}/$ cm at $30\%$ RH to the maximum value of $4.2\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ at $98\%$ RH. The PXRD test confirmed that the structure of the MOF did not change before and after the AC impedance analysis, which also indicated that the proton transfer was carried out in the MOF rather than the product of decomposition. The remarkable $\upsigma$ value under humid environment can be attributed to the acid-base pairs. DMA units can offer easily dissociable $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ in humid air. At the same time, the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules in the channels can reform a more abundant H-bonded network with DMA and sulfate units to facilitate the fast transmission of protons. Thus, high proton concentration and efficient proton transport channels result in high proton conductivity of this MOF. In short, a simple solvothermal mannar was adopted to prepare a stable MOF with excellent proton conductivity at both anhydrous conditions and humid conditions, which affords a useful example for further study of such conductive materials.
In 2016, Furukawa and coworkers also presented a 3D anionic framework, $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{DMA})_{4}$ (VNU-15) including benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate (BDC) and aphthalene-2,6- dicarboxylate (NDC) ligands, and exhibiting an exceptional fob topology [132] which denoted a high $\upsigma$ value at low humidity. Single crystal X-ray data manifested that $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{N D C}}^{2-}$ ligands joined iron cations to form the rod-like SUBs, these SUBs were further connected by sulphate units to build up a 3D structure with 1D pores (size: $2.52\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{\bar{2}}$ . DMA caions orderly lined the 1D channel by H-bond interactions with the $S0_{4}^{2-}$ anions resulting in convenient proton conduction paths. This MOF exhibits thermal-stable up to $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and has a high water uptake of $128\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ as $P/P_{0}$ being 0.60 at room temperature. Following this, VNU-15 obtained excellent $\upsigma$ value of $2.9\times10^{-2}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under $60\%$ RH and $95^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}$ , which was 2.5-fold higher than Nafion under the same conditions, which can keep a constant for more than $40\mathrm{~h~}$ without any appreciable loss. Its $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is $0.22\mathrm{eV}$ at $25\mathrm{-}95^{\circ}C$ and $60\%$ RH, indicating that the proton transfer of this MOF obeys the Grotthuss mechanism.
In 2015, Ghosh group synthesized a 1D anionic In-based MOF, $[\{\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mu{-}\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{4}\}{\cdot}(\mathrm{(LH)}_{4}\}{\cdot}\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ ( $\mathrm{LH~=~}$ protonated melamine) [133]. This MOF contains 1D anionic polymeric chains, which are further hydrogen-bonded with protonated aromatic amines and free $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units forming a 3D network with 1D pores along the $b$ - axis. Notably, these H-bonds related to the anionic chains, LH units, and free water molecules may facilitate proton conducting in this material. Its proton conductive behaviors were measured by EIS technology under different RH and temperature. As expected, the $\upsigma$ value of this MOF is dependent on humidity. When RH reaches $98\%$ RH, the maximum $\upsigma$ of $4.4\times10^{-5}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ can be obtained at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of 0.32 eV evidences that the hopping mechanism is executing for proton conduction. Besides, the authors further assessed the $\upsigma$ value under ${\sf D}_{2}0$ atmosphere, and discovered that the value changed to a low value of $1.01\times10^{-5}$ $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ at $98\%$ RH. This again confirms that water-assisted proton conduction occurred in this MOF.
In 2015, Yaghi et al. reported a 3D porous iron-catecholate MOF, $(\mathrm{Fe(THO).Fe(SO_{4})(D M A)_{3}}$ (Fe-CAT-5), $\mathrm{THO^{6-}=}$ triphenylene-2,3,6, 7,10,11-hexakis(olate)) with ultrahigh proton conductivity [134] which is a double-interpenetrated anionic framework with srs topology. In this MOF, each organic ligand linked three octahedral iron ions to make up a 3D networks. Furthermore, these two networks are bridged by $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ cluster to generate a 3D framework containing 1D pores filled with DMA cations. The MOF has high thermal stability up to $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ and high water uptake capacity $(235~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ as $P/P_{0}=0.9\$ ). AC impedance spectra of FeCAT-5 exhibit that the $\upsigma$ value is distinct dependent on RH, meaning that the $\upsigma$ increases with the increasing RH. This led to maximum $\upsigma$ value of $5.0\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $98\%$ RH and $25~^{\circ}C$ with a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.24~\mathrm{eV}$ . The experimental results reveal that the $\upsigma$ value is not only affected by the $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ content, but also by DMA and sulfate units.
## 2.2. MOFs modified by post-synthetic methods
Recently, post-synthetic methods covering postsynthetic oxidatinon, postsynthetic modification or postsynthetic ligandexchange and so on have gradually become an exceedingly helpful tactic for systemically functionalized MOFs by modifying linkers, metal-nodes, pore characteristics and surface environments to improve structural stability and obtain ideal performance. The relevant sulfonate MOFs is summarized in detail in Table 2.
Strong acid (eg. $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ ) is easy to release protons and to establish H-bonded networks with other components inside the framework thus achieving high proton conductivity. Given Nafion with its terminal $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups denoting high performance, it is feasible to incorporate the $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups into the framework for excellent proton conductivity.
The porous $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ functionalized MOF, $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{3}0.$ –(BDC${\sf S O}_{3}{\sf H})_{2}({\tt B D C}{\cdot}{\sf S O}_{3})$ , $(\mathbf{MIL-101-SO_{3}H})$ was previously reported by Jiang group in 2014 [135] which features a 3D MTN-type zeolite structure, involving two types of similar mesoporous cages filled with $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ sites and $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. Considering its high chemical stability, Lan and coworkers loaded $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ into the pores to obtain a water-stable MOF, $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101-S O_{3}H}$ , which denoted a synergetic superior conductivity with excellent chemical stability [136]. The research combines the $\mathrm{-}S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups fixed in the framework and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ molecules encapsulated strategies (Fig. 11). For comparison, the proton conductivities of $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101-S O_{3}H}$ $(3~\mathrm{~M~})$ , $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101}$ (3 M), and MIL-101- $\mathbf{\delta_{SO_{3}H}}$ are listed as 1.82, $6.09\times10^{-1}$ , and $6.32\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $70\ \textdegree C\_{90\%}$ RH, respectively. Apparently, the high $\upsigma$ of $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101-S O_{3}H}$ (3 M) represented a considerable break through in crystalline solid conductive materials. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values for them are 0.39, 0.33 and $0.27\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively, suggesting that the proton conduction in them all belongs to the Grotthuss mechanism. The superior conductivity was due to the synergistic effect of high-density $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, absorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ encapsulated into pores. Interestingly, $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101-S O_{3}H}$ (3 M) can maintain an excellent $\upsigma$ of $9.2\times10^{-3}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $-40^{\circ}C$ for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ with a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ $(0.21\ \mathrm{eV})$ . This indicates that proton transport is mainly controlled by hopping mechanism at low temperature. In conclusion, MIL101- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ treated with sulfuric acid displayed ultra-high proton conductivity and high structural stability under harsh conditions, laying a foundation for future applications in fuel cells.
Hereafter, Hupp team extended the applicability of this dual strategy by grafting propyl-sulfonic based units on the terephthalate linker of the mesoporous cage-like MIL-101(Cr) [137]. Lately, an interesting MOF, MIL-101(Cr)–NH– $(\mathbf{CH_{2}})_{3}\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ was presented by Maurin and coworkers [138] which was prepared by twosteps post-synthesis modification procedure. First, the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ units of MIL- $(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{-NO_{2}}$ were reduced to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ units to produce MIL$(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{-NH_{2}}$ . And then, the ring opening reaction between 1,3- propanesultone and the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ unit led to the product of MIL-101 (Cr)–NH– $\mathbf{\langleCH_{2}\rangle_{3}S O_{3}H}$ . The PXRD determinations illustrated that both MIL-101(Cr)–NH– $\mathbf{\nabla\cdot(CH_{2})_{3}S O_{3}H}$ and MIL-101(Cr) had similar structures. Nitrogen adsorption isotherms emerged that the pore volume of the functionalized product was $0.699~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . This product can hold stable until $180~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ AC impedance measurements revealed that MIL-101(Cr)–NH– $(\mathbf{CH_{2}})_{3}\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ exhibited the $\upsigma$ value increase with the increase of RH, and obtained the highest $\upsigma$ of $4.80\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ under $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, with a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.26\ \mathrm{~eV}.$ , which reflected the beneficial roles of the $\scriptstyle-\mathrm{NH}(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{3}$ chains bearing the $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups in the proton conduction. Furthermore, the $\upsigma$ value of the modified MOF was significantly improved after impregnated in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . Two kinds of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ aqueous solutions of $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ and $1\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}$ were adopted to soak MIL-101(Cr)–NH– $(\mathbf{CH_{2}})_{3}\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ . The MOF preserved its structural integrity after the strong acid treatment. The rapid decrease of ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorbed amount in $\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}(1\ \mathbf{M})@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{0}1(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2})_{3}S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ verified that the sulfuric acid unit were assuredly located in the pores. Electrochemical tests manifested that the proton conductivity of the MOFs treated with high or low concentration of sulfuric acid would boost slowly with the increase of temperature, and the proton conductivity of $\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}(1\ \mathbf{M})@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}1\mathbf{0}1(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2})_{3}S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ was only about three times higher than that of $\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}(\mathbf{0.1}\ \mathbf{M})@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-101}(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2})_{3}$ $\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ , but far greater than that of MIL-101(Cr)–NH– $(\mathbf{CH_{2}})_{3}\mathbf{SO_{3}H}$ . For $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}(1}&{{}\mathbf{M})@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{10}\mathbf{1}(\mathbf{Cr})\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{2})_{3}S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}}\end{array}$ , its best $\upsigma$ of $1.3\times10^{-1}\mathrm{S/cm}$ under $90\ \%$ RH can be obtained with a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $0.17\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . Similar to the sulnuric acid treated MOF, $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ - $\mathbf{SO_{4}@M I L-101-S O_{3}H}$ mentioned above [136] the H-bonding networks inside $\begin{array}{r l r}{{\bf H}_{2}S{\bf O}_{4}(1}&{{}}&{{\bf M})@{\bf M}\mathrm{IL}-101(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{NH}-(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{3}S{\bf O}_{3}\mathrm{H}}\end{array}$ established via $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are vely conducive to the efficient transmission of protons.
The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF, UiO-66 is a promising candidate for the preparation of proton conductor with high efficiency due to its highly water and chemical stable features [139–141]. It is composed of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ clusters linked to each other by 1,4- benzene-dicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ ligands. It showed higher surface-area and pore-volume, and its framework can be expediently functionalized without destroying the structural integrity, by either employing modified $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ ligand as starting reagent or by postsynthetic modification or postsynthetic ligand-exchange.
A tactic using a post-synthetic oxidation to produce $\mathbf{-}\pmb{S}\pmb{0}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ groups in the backbone of the MOFs was reported by Hong group [142]. They achieved an excellent proton-conducting MOF, UiO${\bf66}({\bf50_{3}H})_{2}$ by postsynthetic oxidation from UiO- ${\bf\nabla}{\cdot}{\bf66}({\bf5H})_{2}$ without destroying its framwork. Firstly, $\mathbf{UiO-66(SH)}_{2}$ is made by microwave-reaction of $Z\mathrm{{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ with 2,5-dimercapto-1,4-benzenedicar boxylic-acid in DMF. Thereafter, $\mathbf{UiO-66(SH)_{2}}$ was oxidized via $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under acidic condition to generate $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ functionalized UiO-66 $(\pmb{S0}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ . Like UiO-66, UiO-66- $(\mathbf{S0_{3}H})_{2}$ and UiO-66- $(\mathbf{SH})_{2}$ keep their thermal stability up to $400~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and display excellent water stability after dipping in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ for thirty days and in boiling $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ . As the steric bulkiness of the functional units filled in the pores of the MOFs increases, the BET surface areas of UiO-66, UiO-66- $(\mathbf{SH})_{2}$ and UiO-66- $(\mathbf{S0_{3}H})_{2}$ decreases accordingly, which are 897, 308 and $35~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. Water vapor adsorption experiments also affirmed that UiO-66- $(\pmb{S0}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ had higher hydrophilicity than UiO-66 and UiO-66- $(\mathbf{SH})_{2}$ . The $\upsigma$ values of the three MOFs reinforced significantly above $50\%$ RH. UiO-66 $(\mathbf{SH})_{2}$ and UiO-66 manifested general $\upsigma$ of $2.5\ \times\ 10^{-5}$ and $4.3\times10^{-6}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ under $80~^{\circ}C$ and $90\%$ RH, respectively. The $\upsigma$ of UiO-66 $(\pmb{S0}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ was significantly improved to $8.4\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under the same environment. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values are 0.43, 0.23 and $0.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ for UiO-66, $\mathbf{UiO-66(SH)_{2}}$ and UiO- ${\bf66}({\bf50_{3}H})_{2}$ , respectively. Obviously, the proton transport of the latter two MOFs follows a hopping mechanism, that is, the protons are quickly transported along the $-\mathsf{S O}_{3}^{-}...\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{O}^{+}...(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{\mathrm{n}}$ pathway. Note that the prominent proton conductive properties of UiO-66 $(\pmb{S0}_{3}\mathbf{H})_{2}$ obtained by the post-oxidation method is due to the fact that the sulfonic groups introduced can not only provide high concentration protons, but also enhance the hydrophilicity of the channels, which is conducive to the establishment of efficient H-bonded transmission channels, thus resulting in high proton conductivity.
In 2019, Das and coworkers described two post-synthetically modified UiO-66-based sulfonate MOFs (PSM-1 and PSM-2) with different side-arm chain lengths, and inspected the influence of their architectonic differences on proton conductivity [143]. PSM-1 and PSM-2 were fabricated via UiO-66- ${\bf{\cdot N H}}_{2}$ reaction with 1,3-propane sultone or 1,4-butane sultone. Both modified MOFs have similar PXRD patterns to UiO-66 implying the similar structures. The crystallinity and integrity of PSM 1 and PSM 2 could maintain after placing in open air, at high temperature or high pressure, and in boiling water for seven days. Under the same humidity as $P/P_{0}$ being 0.5, the water uptake of PSM 1 and PSM 2 is largely higher than that of UiO-66- ${\bf N H}_{2}$ suggesting that high hydrophilicity of the modified MOFs is very favorable for establishing continuous H-bonded networks. PSM-1 possesses shorter suspended alkyl-chain containing $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups than PSM-2, which results in significant difference in proton conductivity. PSM-1 exhibited an excellent $\upsigma$ of $1.64\times10^{-1}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $80~\textdegree C-95\%$ RH. PSM-2 evidenced a lower $\upsigma$ of $4.6\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ under the same conditions. Moreover, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of them are calculated to be 0.11 and $0.29\mathrm{eV}$ respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of PSM-1 is lower than that of PSM-2, indicating the obvious influence of easy acquisition of sulfonic-group protons. Although the reason for the great difference in proton conductivity caused by such a minor structural difference is fuzzy, the strategy of introducing sulfonic groups to MOFs through post-modification to obtain high proton conductivity is worthy of further applications.
Lan and his colleagues reported a meaningful and practicable method to gradually improve proton conductivity, that is, to decorate different proton sources and jumping sites on UiO-66- ${\bf N H}_{2}$ (Fig. 12) [144]. The stable UiO-66- $\mathbf{NH}_{2}$ was firstly prepared as the starting meterial. And then half of the organic ligands (BDC$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ) in $\mathbf{UiO-G6-NH_{2}}$ are replaced by 2-sulfo-terephthalic acid to give the new MOF, UiO-66-AS. Subsequently, the remaining noncoordinated amino groups react with the –CHO on the imidazole group to generate IM-UiO-66-AS. The PXRD determinations denoted that UiO-66-AS and IM-UiO-66-AS preserved the host framework and porosity of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . Additionally, IM-UiO-66- AS kept the structure intact after immersed in different $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solutions (pH being 1, 3 and 5). By carefully comparing their structures, it can be found that IM-UiO-66-AS has the most proton sources and hopping sites, so it must show the optimal proton conductivity. Thus, IM-UiO-66-AS has an especial $\upsigma$ of $1.54\times10^{-1}\mathrm{S}/$ cm under $80~^{\circ}C$ and $98\%$ RH with a lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ $(0.20~\mathrm{eV})$ . Its timedependent performance revealed a exceptional stability more than $100\mathrm{~h~}$ . The $\upsigma$ values of UiO-66-AS and UiO-66- ${\bf N H}_{2}$ are $1.7\times10^{-4}$ and $3\times10^{-6}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{cm}}$ , respectively, which are significantly lower than those of IM-UiO-66-AS under the same conditions. The wonderful proton conductive performance of IM-UiO-66-AS can be attributed to that the $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units are easy to offer protons and adsorb $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ to form H-bonds for fast proton transportation. Besides, the attached Im units also act as proton sources and afford jumping sites, resusting in further enhancement of $\upsigma$ values.
A water-stable porous 3D Ti-based MOF, $\mathrm{Ti}_{12}0_{15}(\mathrm{mdip})_{3}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}$ (MIP-177-LT) (m $\mathrm{Iip}=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetracarboxydiphenylmethane) was synthesized in 2018 [145] in which $\mathrm{Ti}_{12}0_{15}$ oxo-clusters were connected by mdip units and $\mathtt{C O O^{-}}$ anions forming a series of hexagonal 1D pores (diameter: ${\sim}1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). Taking account of its distinct structural characteristics, Devautour-Vinot and coworkers soaked MIP-177-LT into $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ solution to generate MIP-177- ${\bf\cal S O_{4}H}$ -LT [146]. In the new derivative, some formates were substituted by hydrogen-sulfate groups to constitute Brønsted-acid sites, which maintained the integrity of structure and porosity of the parent MOF. MIP-177- ${\bf\cal S O_{4}H}$ -LT has a superior $\upsigma$ of $2.6\times10^{-2}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ under $25~\%$ RH, which is greatly higher than that of MIP-177-LT $(4.3\times10^{-6}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ . The results reveal again that the ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ treatment is a particularly effective approach for enhancing more acidic proton sources in the MOFs. In addition, the structural integrity of MIP-177- $\mathbf{SO_{4}H}$ -LT can be kept before and after AC determinations, and the highest $\upsigma$ value can be maintained over seven days at 25 $^\circ C/95\%$ RH. The proton conduction mechanism is further expounded on the atomic-scale via AIMD simulation. A Grotthustype mechanism whereby protons are transferred from the $S O_{4}\mathrm{H}$ units to the adsorbed water units in MIP-177- ${\bf\cal S O_{4}H}$ -LT, and then the protons travel across the H-bonding networks established by the guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units and sulfnate units along the pores.
Recently, Zang group fabricated a Cr-based MOF (Cr-MIL-88B) with chromium instead of ferrum in MIL-88B framework [147] to improve hydrothermal stability [148]. Moreover, Cr-MIL-88B was postfunctionalized through immobilizing 3-pyridine sulfonic-acid and 2-(4-pyridyl)ethane sulfonic-acid on the uncoordinated chromium units, generating two meaningful derivatices, Cr-MIL-88B-PSA and Cr-MIL-88B-PESA (Fig. 13). Importantly, chromium units on the host-framework are decorated through - $S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, which extend along the large-size pores to the center of the pore, resulting in a well-organized dense sulfonic groups as proton source. Under $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $85\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ value of Cr-MIL-88B-PSA is $1.58\times10^{-1}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ , and is higher than that of Cr-MIL-88B-PESA $(4.50\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ , both which are significantly higher than that of Cr-MIL-88B $(6.0\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ under the same conditions. The difference in the proton conductivity of the former two MOFs can be explained by the difference in the number of $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units in the structures. Note that there are $1.52\ -{\cal S}0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ per unit in Cr-MIL-88B-PSA and 1.45 $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ per unit in Cr-MIL88B-PESA. In addition, $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of 2-(4-pyridyl)ethanesulfonicacid (5.68) and 3-pyridinesulfonicacid (3.12) are different suggesting that Cr-MIL-88B-PSA has a greater ability to ionize H-ions than CrMIL-88BPESA. At $85\%$ RH, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of two MOFs were estimated to be no more than $0.4\mathrm{eV}$ , suggesting a Grotthuss conducting mechanism.
A porous $Z\mathrm{r}^{\mathrm{IV}}.$ -based MOF ${\bf z_{r}}$ -TNDC) with methylthio groups provides a platform for postsynthetic modification [149]. On this basis, He and coworkers described a $Z\mathrm{r}^{\mathrm{IV}}.$ -based MOF $\mathbf{(H@Zr-}$ TNDC-Ox) obtained by postsynthetic oxidation and acidification by sulfuric acid [150]. $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra approved that the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{S C H}_{3}}$ units in ${\bf z_{r}}$ -TNDC were nearly oxidized to sulfoxide or sulfone units (certain ratio being 5:7). The intact structure of the oxidation product is similar to the parent MOF as verified by PXRD patterns. So, the authors soaked Zr-TNDC-Ox in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ aqueous solution for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ to get an acidic framework $\mathbf{H@Zr}$ -TNDC-Ox that maintained good crystallinity. The $\upsigma$ values of the three MOFs were explored under various temperatures and different RHs. The proton conductivity of all complexes soars with enhancing temperature or humidity. $\mathbf{H@Zr}$ -TNDC-Ox denoted the highest $\upsigma$ value of $3.16\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $90~\textdegree C-95\%$ RH, which is great higher than that of Zr-TNDC and of Zr-TNDC-Ox. Apparently, the protophilic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units, sulfoxide, sulfone and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ in $\mathbf{H@Zr}$ -TNDC-Ox can act as proton carriers, and construct ample H-bonded pathways for rapid proton transfer resulting in a high $\upsigma$ value. The proton transfer of H@Zr-TNDC-Ox obeys the Grotthuss mechanism as $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ being $0.25\mathrm{eV}.$ . It emphasized that it is an effective method of using post-oxidation and acidification to improve proton conductivity.
A 3D Al-based MOF, [Al(OH)(BDC-Br)] (CAU-10) (B $){\mathsf{C}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Br}}^{2-}=5.$ - bromo-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate) is built up from helical chains of cis corner-sharing ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{6}$ octahedra inter-joined by isophthalate ions [151] which features two types of square-shaped pores. The minimum diameter of these pores is $1.1{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}},$ and the maximum diameter is $6.6\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ The combination of the properties of Al-MOFs and sulfofunctionalized MOFs attracted the much attention. Stock and coworkers prepared 10 mixed-linker MOFs $\mathsf{I A l}(\mathsf{O H})(m\mathrm{-BDC-X})_{1}.$ - $\mathrm{\Delta_{y}(m{-}B D C{-}S O_{3}H)_{y}l}$ $\mathsf{\Pi}_{m-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}}~=~1,3$ -benzene-dicarboxylicacid; X $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathrm{H}\mathbf{\tau}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ , OH) showing the CAU-10-type structures [152]. Three types of MOFs are prepared by using $m{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-X}$ and m$\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDCSO}_{3}\mathrm{Li}$ with different ratios. The obtained compounds are expressed as CAU-10-H/Sx, -N/Sx and $\mathbf{\nabla-0}/\mathbf{Sx}.$ appearing excellent thermal stability until $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The proton conductivity of CAU10-O/S2, CAU-10-N/S2 and CAU-10-H/S1 are measured due to the presence of $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups. The low $\upsigma$ values were observed in CAU-10 analogs, except Al-MIL-53- $\mathbf{oso_{3}H}$ [153] that was reported by Kapteijn group and demonstrated a high $\upsigma$ of $\sim5.88\times10^{-3}\:\mathrm{S}/$ cm at $68\mathrm{~\textperthousand~}$ and anhydrous conditions. The low $\upsigma$ of about $10^{-6}–10^{-7}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ $(57-140~^{\circ}C)$ for CAU-10-O/S2, $10^{-7}–10^{-8}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ $(100-150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for CAU-10-N/S2, and $10^{-7}\:\:\:\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}\:\:\:(140\:\:^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ for CAU-10-H/S1, respectively. The number of $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups in all materials was basically the same, so the action of $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathsf{H}.}\end{array}$ , $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ and –OH groups affected the behavior of proton conductance.
Considering that the $\mathbf{Cr(III)}$ terephthalate MOF, MIL-101 can withstand strong acids, Ponomareva and coworkers immersed MIL-101 into aqueous $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ solution $(2.7\:\mathrm{M})$ to prepare the hybrid material, $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101}$ [154] in 2012. To avert deterioration of the $\upsigma$ value deriving from the extended microstructures of water, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ with a high boiling-point is the best candidate. The wrapping of nonvolatile acid was reversible and the acid can be rinsed off by $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , while maintaining the integrity of the original structure. AC impedance determinations were performed adopting a two-probe approach. The results illustrated that $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@}$ -MIL101 exhibited a high $\upsigma$ value of $6.0\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ at $20~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}/20\%$ RH, which slightly increased to $6.0\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ and decreased as temperature above $80~^{\circ}C$ indicating that the loss of lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules had a prominent impact on its proton conductivity. Excitingly, this material still denoted a high $\upsigma$ of $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and low RH of $0.13\%$ , ranking high among the MOF-based materials. This study highlighted the viability of increasing the foreign Brønsted acid to strengthen proton conductivity.
After two years, another two stronger acids, e.g. p$\mathrm{CH_{3}C_{6}H_{4}S O_{3}H}$ or $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ were incorporated into MIL-101 to improve the proton conductivity (Fig. 14) [155]. These acid $\pmb{\mathcal{Q}}$ MIL products were obtained by simply soaking MIL-101 in $4.12\mathrm{~M~}$ aqueous solutions of $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $2.2\ \mathrm{M}\ p{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CH_{3}C_{6}H_{4}}S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , respectively. They have good stability and maintain structural integrity after multiple tests or long-term heating. The $\mathbf{CF_{3}S O_{3}H@M I L-101}$ hybrid material demonstrated the super-high $\upsigma$ of $8\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ (RT, $100\%$ RH) and $5\times10^{-2}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ (RT, $15\%$ RH), comparable to any commercial PEM materials. At $100\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ value for $\pmb{p}\ll $ - $\mathbf{CH_{3}C_{6}H_{4}S O_{3}H@M L-101}$ is $1.8\times10^{-5}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and slightly boosts to $3.0\times10^{-5}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Note that changing the temperature and humidity does not observably affect the $\upsigma$ value, since the reserved acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ units can hold in the protonconduction networks. The $\upsigma$ value of $p{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{C}_{6}\mathbf{H}_{4}S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{10}\mathbf{1}$ is much smaller than $\mathbf{CF_{3}S O_{3}H@M I L-101}$ , which can be interpreted as low acid content, $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ value, and water content. Acid medium may form large separation droplets and coexist in MIL-101. On the one hand, the proton transfer seemed to obey the Grotthusslike mechanism through rich hydrogen-bonds; on the other hand, it appeared in the successive defragmentation process of droplet to ensure the long-term transfer of protons along the hybrid material.
In 2014, Hong group immersed a porpous MOF, $[\mathsf{N i}_{2}(\mathsf{d o b d c})$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}0\right)_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ $(\mathbf{Ni_{2}}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ (dobdc $^{\cdot4-}=2{,}5$ -dioxido-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate) into ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ aqueous solutions, and examined the pH-dependent proton conductive behavior in this MOF [158]. $\mathbf{Ni_{2}}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ possesses 1D hexagonal-pore with a diameter of $11\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Fig. 15), which remains intact at pH values as low as 1.8. This material revealed excellent stability in hot-water for seven days. After this material was immersed in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution with different acidity, the treated $\mathbf{H}^{+}@\mathbf{Ni}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH}=1.8\dot{\bf\Phi},$ revealed an outstanding $\upsigma$ of $2.2\times10^{-2}\ {\mathsf{S}}/{\mathsf{c m}}$ under $80~^{\circ}C$ $195\%$ RH, which was two orders of magnitude greater than that of $\mathbf{Ni_{2}}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{(\upsigma}}&{{}=\mathrm{1.4~\times~}10^{-4}~\mathrm{S/cm})}\end{array}$ under the identical conditions. The $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ immersed in the system was firmly fixed in the pores because the supernatant of the treated MOF immersed in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was nearly neutral. At the same time, ${\mathsf{N i}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions exuded from the system were also detected, indicating that the partial ligands existed in the channel in the form of anions. The IR spectroscopy under different pH conditions evidenced that the protonated water clusters played an important role in the migration of protons through pores. These data demonstrated the proton conduction is carried out through the Grotthus-like mechanism.
In 2018, Jeong team prepared a Co-based MOF, Co-MOF-74 using ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and H4dobdc ligand, and determined its $\upsigma$ values after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ treatment [159]. More importantly, the proton transport in three directions was studied in detail by using a single crystal sample. Co-MOF-74 contains 1D channels along c-axis, which are isolated by framework walls. After ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ $\mathrm{\underline{{\PH}}}=3\mathrm{\cdot}$ treatment, the maximum $\upsigma$ of the single crystal sample in $c$ -axis was $4.5\times10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{c m}$ at $90~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ under $95\%$ RH. Significantly, the single-crystal $\upsigma$ in $c$ -axis $(2.1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ at $30\%$ RH is enhanced by 1200 times compared to the $a$ -axis conductivity $(1.7\times10^{-6}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ , indicating the directed proton transfer in the pores. The lower $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $0.12\mathrm{eV}$ for the $c$ -axis further demonstrated the proton conduction obeyed the Grotthuss mechanism in $c$ -axis. The authors pointed out that proton conductivity is related to proton concentration and proton mobility. The high $c$ -axis $\upsigma$ value can be attributed to the high concentration protons that can shift rapidly by isolated channels without interference.
Incorporating ionic liquids (ILs) in porous MOFs to fabricate ILsMOF nanocomposites has proved to be a feasible method for realizing new high-performance anhydrous proton conductors. Because the ILs have prominent features, such as good thermal stability, nonvolatility, nonflammability and low corrosivity. In 2017, Wan Group reported a proton conductive compound, (EIMSHTFSA@MIL), which was synthesized by soaking MIL-101 into binary iILs, EIMS-HTFSA $\mathbf{\dot{\tau}}_{\mathrm{EIMS}}=1$ -(1-ethyl-3-imidazolio)propane-3- sulfonate, HTFSA $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ N,N-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)amide) [160]. The framework of MIL-101 can be maintained after EIMSHTFSA loading as evidenced by PXRD determinations. EIMSHTFSA@MIL illustrated high thermal stability up to $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The $\upsigma$ value of EIMS-HTFSA@MIL smoothly increased with the increasing temperature, and exhibited the highest value of $1.17\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/$ cm at $140^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ which was about $10^{6}$ higher than that of the original MIL-101. The conduction mechanism was revealed as the protonated $-S{\cal O}_{3}\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{HTFSA}$ and non-protonated $-S0_{3}^{-}{-}\mathrm{TFSA^{-}}$ served as proton-source and proton transfer sites, respectively, which made proton jump among $-S0_{3}^{-}$ -TFSA1 sites, thus realizing efficient proton transmission.
Later, the same group continously introduced a battery of ILs with different acid counteranions $\left(\mathrm{R-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}\right),$ into MIL-101 to generate several hybrid materials, SA-EIMS-MIL-101, MSA-EIMS-MIL101 and PTSA-EIMS-MIL-101 ( $S\mathsf{A}=$ sulfateacid, $\mathrm{\Delta{MSA}=}$ methanesul fonateacid, $\mathrm{{PTSA}}={\mathfrak{p}}.$ -toluenesulfonateacid) [161]. These hybrid materials exhibited anhydrous conductive properties with longterm durability even heated in air at $150~^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . At $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , $\upsigma$ values for SA-EIMS-MIL-101, MSA-EIMS-MIL-101 and PTSA-EIMS-MIL101 are $1.89\times10^{-3}$ , $1.02\times10^{-4}$ and $2.78\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ , respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values for them are 0.26, 0.30 and 0.42 eV, respectively. Anion volumes of $\mathsf{R}{-}\mathsf{S}0_{3}^{-}$ showed remarkable effects on the $\upsigma$ of these hybrid ILs-MOF materials (SA-EIMS-MIL-101 $>$ MSAEIMS- MIL-101 $>$ PTSA-EIMS-MIL-101). These works suggest that the combination of multiple integrated ILs and MOF frameworks will afford high proton transmission.
## 2.3. Composite membranes incorporating sulfonate MOFs
As mentioned above, most sulfonate MOFs exhibit good structural stability, especially high proton conductivity, but the brittleness and unprocessability of these materials limit their further applications. In addition, pure organic polymers have the advantages of good mechanical properties and easy processing, but their proton conductivity needs to be improved. Therefore, people have been thinking about combining the advantages of both them to prepare novel hybrid membranes doped with sulfonate MOFs for future practical applications. As preparation for Nafion, incorporating $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups into organic polymers is a facile and effective method for constructing efficient PEMs. The sulfonatefunctionalized MOFs can provide more $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ sites for PEMs. Besides, these MOFs can also combine with $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ units at the boundaries to afford additional transport ways for proton conducting. The relevant MOFs materials are summarized in Table 3.
In 2017, Zang group doped the pristine MOF, MIL-101, the – $S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ modified MOF, MIL-101- $\mathbf{\delta_{S}0_{3}H}$ , and non-volatile acid-loaded MOFs, acids@MIL-101 (acids: $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\dot{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ ) into chitosan (CS) respectively to obtain a series of composite membranes, and compared the properties of these composite membranes, especially the proton conductivity [162]. According to experimental data, the good compatibility between these MIL-101-based materials and CS substrate due to mutual effect of H-bonds can greatly enhance the $\upsigma$ values, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ , thermal and mechanical properties of the composite membranes. It is found that the proton conductivity of the composite membrane is related to the amount of filler. For example, when the filling-quantity of $\mathbf{H_{2}S O_{4}@M I L-101}$ increased from $4\%$ to $8\%$ , the $\upsigma$ value of $\mathbf{CS}/\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{10}1$ boosted from $1.3\ \times\ 10^{-2}$ to $1.5\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at 2 $5\ \mathrm{{^circC}}/100\%$ RH. When the filling-quantity elevated to $12\%$ , the $\upsigma$ value decreased to $1.2\times10^{-2}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ , which is mainly due to the large surface area of MIL-101 and its tendency to agglomerate. With the temperature increasing, $\mathbf{CS}/\mathbf{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{MIL101}.8$ attained the maximum $\upsigma$ of $9.5\ \times\ 10^{-2}\ \mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $100\%$ RH), which is close to that $(9.4\times10^{-2}\:\mathrm{S/cm})$ of $\mathbf{CS}/\mathbf{CF_{3}S O_{3}H}$ -MIL-101–10. Obviously, $-\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}$ and $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ units are good proton acceptors, providing sufficient medium for proton transfer. Also, the optimal $\upsigma$ value of CS/MIL-101- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H-4}$ can reach $6.4\times10^{-2}\ 5/\mathrm{cm}$ under the same conditions, which is smaller than that of the former two composite membranes, possibly because the positions of hydrophilic $-S0_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups are fixed and the distance between them is not conducive to the formation of a continuous proton transport channel. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of $\mathrm{CS}/\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}101{-}8,$ $\mathbf{CS}/\mathbf{CF}_{3}\mathbf{SO}_{3}\mathbf{H@MIL-101-10}$ and CS/MIL101- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H_{-4}}$ are 0.181, 0.179, and $0.174\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, which are ascribed to Grotthuss-type mechanism. In a word, the synergistic effects of the affinity between MIL-101-based materials and CS due to H-bonding interactions, H-bonds involved in the components in these MOFs, and high proton concentration carried by the post-synthetic modification lead to the excellent proton conductivity and good mechanical properties of these composite films.
As early as 2014, Jiang team tried to dop a sulfonated MOF, MIL$\mathbf{101-SO_{3}}$ that was obtained by treating MIL-101 with concentrated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and trifluoromethanesulfonic anhydride in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ into the sulfonated polyether etherketone (SPEEK) substrate to fabricate hybrid membranes [163]. The PXRD measurements confirm that MIL-101- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S0_{3}}$ has the same structure as its parent MIL-101. Both of them are thermally stable until $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Consequently, the thermal stability of hybrid membranes is higher than that of pristine membrane and also increases with the increase of the doping amount of $\bf{M I L-101-S O_{3}}$ . Similarly, the methanol permeability of SPEEK/ MIL- $\mathbf{S0_{3}}–\mathbf{X}$ was reinforced by the loadings. Nevertheless, compared with the pure SPEEK, the MeOH permeability of the composite film was elevated slightly. The $\upsigma$ values of these hybrid membranes were examined under various temperature and $100\%$ RH. The positive temperature $\upsigma$ correlations can be observed. At $75\mathrm{~\}^{\circ}C/100\%$ RH, the $\upsigma$ value of SPEEK/ MIL-101- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{S0_{3}}$ -7.5 is $3.06\times10^{-1}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ , and is 96.2 higher than that of the pure film. The excellent $\upsigma$ of SPEEK/ MIL-101- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{s0_{3}}\mathbf{-}7.5$ is mainly attributed to the proton hopping sites provided by sulfonic units, the formation of additional conduction pathways among the interfaces of membrane and sulfonated MOF, and the construction of Hbonded network by hydrolysis of coordination unsaturated metals. A series of MIL-101- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H}/$ Nafion composite membranes with different doping contents of MIL-101- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{s}\mathbf{0}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ were prepared by Yin group in 2019 [164]. The relationship between the doping amount of MOFs, free volume and gas permeability of the composite membranes was mainly discussed. The authors found that gas permeability reduces sharply with the increase of doping amount of MOFs. The MIL-101- $\mathbf{\delta\calS0_{3}H}$ -12/Nafion showed an enhanced $\upsigma$ of $1.98\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{S/cm}}$ at $109^{\circ}\mathsf C$ $1100\%$ RH. After $110^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ the $\upsigma$ value of the membranes decreased sharply due to the rapid evaporation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules. The improvement of $\upsigma$ of MIL-101- $\mathbf{SO_{3}H}_{I}$ Nafion composite membranes was mainly due to the construction of protons enriched areas and ample H-bonds with $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ units among the interfaces of Nafion-substrate and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL-101-SO_{3}H}.}\end{array}$
In 2018, Na and coworkers [165] firstly obtained a new aminosulfo-bifunctionalized MOF, MIL-101- $\mathbf{NH_{2}}\mathbf{-SO_{3}H}$ (namely MNS) (Fig. 16), which was prepared by a hydrothermal reaction and postmodification. Continuously, MNS was loaded into a sulfonated hybrid poly(arylene ether ketone) (SNF-PAEK) matrix to generate hybrid membranes. They discovered that the mechanical strength and MeOH permeability of the composite membrane were enhanced due to the interactions between amino units of MNS and sulfonic on the side chain of SNF-SPAEK. Interestingly, with the increase additon of MNS, the $\upsigma$ first elevated and then reduced. For example, with the increase of MNS amount from 0 to $3\%$ , the $\upsigma$ varied from $5.5\times10^{-2}$ to $8.0\times10^{-2}\ S/\mathrm{cm}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and from $1.45\times10^{-1}$ to $1.92\times10^{-1}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ Whereafter, the $\upsigma$ decreased from $8.0\times10^{-2}$ to $7.0\times10^{-2}\:\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $1.92\times10^{-1}$ to $1.74\times10^{-1}\:\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $80~^{\circ}C$ as the loading of MNS being $5\%$ . This shows that too much MNS leads to a higher crosslink density of the polymer matrix, which may hinder the movement of protons. Apparently, MNS offers abundant and ordered $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups and rearranges the microstructure of hybrid films helping the efficient proton hopping. Thus, MNS@SNF-PAEK-3 manifests the higher $\upsigma$ value of $1.92\times10^{-1}\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $80~\mathrm{^circC}/100\%$ RH, which is 1.32 times higher than the pure SNF-SPAEK film.
As our group has just reviewed the application of Zr-based compounds, UiO-66 and its sulfonate functionalized MOFs [166–172] in composite membranes, for the sake of brevity, some relevant reviews are omitted here, please refer to the literature [20].
MOF, MIL-53 is constituted by huge inorganic chains, consisting of trans-cornersharing $\{\mathsf{M O}_{4}({\mathsf{O H}},\mathsf{F})_{2}\}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{Al}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathsf{C r}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{III}}$ or $\mathrm{In}^{\mathrm{III}^{\mathrm{\dag}}}$ ) octahedra cross-linked by bisbidentate terephthalate linkers, forming one-dimensional rhombic shaped pores [171,172]. Meanwhile, MIL-53 materials hold high surface area, large pores, and good chemical stability. The amino-functionalized MIL-53(Al)– ${\bf{\cdot N H}}_{2}$ possesses high specific-area and efficient chemical modification with – $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ units, which may synergistically make this material as conductive nanofillers with excellent proton conducting and strength enhancement characteristics for the construction of hybrid PEMs. An approach for preparing sulfonate-functionalized MOFs as protoncon ducting solid electrolytes to fabricated hybrid MOFs with new nature was reported by Molavi team [173]. A sulfonated polymer/MOF hybrid membrane (MOF-g-PAMPS) was fabricated via redox-polymerization of 2-acrylamido-2-methyl-1-propane sulfonic-acid, and grafted onto the surfaces of aminofunctionalized MIL-53(Al)– ${\bf{\cdot N H}}_{2}$ nanoparticles. The postpolymerization modification of MOF- $\mathbf{g}$ -PAMPS hybrid membranes act as sulfonated hydrophilic monomers providing an anhydrous $\upsigma$ of $4.9\times10^{-5}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The ideal proton conductivity of MOF$\pmb{g}$ -PAMPS strongly supported the results that the polymerized sulfonated monomer was a good way to obtain complete MOFs nanoparticles with higher sulfonic-acid function.
## 3. Conclusion and perspectives
The current progress of proton conductive sulfonate MOFs are reviewed herein. From the viewpoint of material design, MOFs offer accurate functional-structural information, designability and variable modulation approaches to facilitate proton transmission. As reviewed above, several strategies can be adopted to improve the proton-conductive properties of these MOFs. Intriguingly, some specially modified MOFs exhibit ultra-high $\upsigma$ values around $10^{-1}{-}1\bar{0}^{-2}\mathsf{S}/\bar{\mathrm{cm}}$ . The following important aspects need to be noted in the future research:
(1) Construction of MOFs with more free sulfonate groups. According to this review, free $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ or $-S0_{3}^{-}$ groups carried in MOFs can not only serve as proton sources but also form dense and ample H-bonded networks with other inherent chemical components or filled functional small molecules (such as water, imidazole, parazole, hydronium and ammonium and son) within the frameworks, which are very conducive to efficient and rapid transport of protons. Therefore, whether the organic ligands with multiple sulfonic acid groups are obtained through the molecular design for the construction of MOFs, or more free sulfonic acid groups are introduced through the post-synthetic modification, we must pay special attention to them in the future research.
(2) Construction of MOFs with high water-stability. For water-assisted proton conductive materials, high water stability is critical and necessary for their performance testing and future applications. Nevertheless, in earlier reports, the investigation on water stability of sulfonate MOFs was limited. As summarized in the previous section, only the water stability of some sulfonate MOFs has been explored in depth, such as the structural robustness after being immersed in water at room terpemature or being kept in boiling water and after being stored in high humidity for a period of time. In general, most sulfonate MOFs exhibit high water stability due to the flexible and strong coordination stability of sulfonate groups and the resulting rich hydrogen bonding systems. There are two recommendations to obtain MOFs with high water stability: (a) Besides with sulfonic acid group on the organic ligand, people should also introduce more of other functional groups (such as carboxylic acid, phosphate group, pyridine, etc., through the synergy between the functional groups to build stable MOFs). (b) It is better to choose metal ions (such as zirconium, chromium, etc.) with high coordination number and hydrophilic properties to build up porous multi-dimensional MOFs.
(3) Preparation more single-crystal sample for protonconducting research. The study of inherent proton transfer in MOFs is still hindered because of the shortage of high quality single-crystal sample. For example, defects within the framework and the effect on proton transmission path are not yet known. At present, the structures of designed and as-synthesized MOFs are more complicated. So, it is difficult to draw a clear conclusion about the correlation between the composition/structure and properties. Computer-aided theoretical computing can be a helpful method and is widely used to calculate the correlation between structure and conductivity, which in turn promotes the discovery of fresh structures. Nevertheless, the complicated structure brings difficulties to the establishment of theoretical models, and puts forward higher demands for computer modeling. Therefore, the perfect combination of single-crystal determination and theoretical calculation will bring new opportunities for the research of proton conduction mechanism and the design of proton conductive materials.
In general, proton conductive sulfonate-based MOFs have made great progress in recent years. The proton-conductive sulfonatebased MOFs can make up the deficiency of Nafion membrane at high-temperature and low-humidity, and will be extensively used in fuel cells and hybrid membranes. This will promote the advance on synthesis and structural design, and provide hopeful directions for proton conductive materials. Over time, the widespread application of sulfonate MOFs with unique functions will be realized in practical applications.
